"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][1]={"university_computer_science_en:M1-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M1-L1 – What is computer science?<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: Foundations of computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand computer science as a\nscientific discipline, distinguish programming from it, and relate the\nconcepts of data, information, algorithms, models, abstraction,\ncomputability and computational cost to real software systems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Behind every program is a model of the world<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a program that manages a large collection of films. On the\nsurface, you see input fields, lists and search results. Underneath,\nfundamental questions arise: What exactly is a film? Does an alternative\nlanguage version belong to the same work? How unambiguous is a title?\nHow do you find an entry quickly without searching through everything?\nWhat happens if two people make changes at the same time?<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_001_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Sequence of problem, model, algorithm, program and evaluation\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: An everyday problem is turned into an abstract\nmodel, then an algorithm and an executable program; verification\ncompletes the workflow.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The choice of programming language alone does not answer these\nquestions. They concern <strong>computer science<\/strong>: the\nsystematic description and processing of information through formal\nmodels and executable procedures. Computer science investigates which\ntasks are computable in principle, which methods work well and how\nreliable technical systems can be built from them.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Programming<\/strong> means expressing a solution in a\nprogramming language. <strong>Software development<\/strong> also\nincludes planning, testing, modification, release and maintenance. Both\nare important practical activities, but neither encompasses the entire\ndiscipline. A computer scientist who is not currently writing a single\nline of code can still analyse algorithms, design a data model or assess\nthe security of an architecture.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Everyday problem: ‘Find a film even if its title differs.’\n                   │\n                   ▼\nDomain model: work, title variants, language, identifier\n                   │\n                   ▼\nProcedure: normalise, search, rank matches\n                   │\n                   ▼\nProgram: user interface + search logic + data store\n                   │\n                   ▼\nEvaluation: correct matches, runtime, error cases, data protection<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is a first guiding principle for the entire course: <strong>What\nmakes a system good is not the number of servers, but the suitability of\nits models and methods for the actual problem.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data is not yet understandable information<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a file contains <code>12, 18, 25, 19<\/code>. These are\ninitially <strong>data<\/strong>: recorded symbols or values. If they are\ninterpreted as temperatures in degrees Celsius on four days, a\nmeaningful context emerges. If instead they are visitor counts or\nprices, their meaning changes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Information<\/strong> is not an independent substance\nautomatically contained in a number. It arises when data is placed in a\nmeaningful context and interpreted. The unit, time, origin and\nmeasurement method can sometimes be just as important as the actual\nvalue.<\/p>\n<p>At a low level, a computer stores only encoded states. Whether a bit\nsequence represents a number, a letter, a pixel or a machine instruction\ndepends on rules and conventions. Even text must be\n<strong>encoded<\/strong>: a character encoding maps characters to\nparticular byte sequences. A database supplements the encoding with a\nschema that describes, among other things, which fields belong\ntogether.<\/p>\n<p>In a media archive, for example, the following information may be\nimportant:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stored value<\/th>\n<th>Understandable only in context<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>2024<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Year of release or file size?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>de<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Language code, country identifier or part of a name?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>000417<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Unique identifier or a number whose leading zeros could be\nomitted?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>3,7<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Rating on which scale and in which decimal format?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A common software problem is therefore not a calculation error but an\n<strong>incorrect interpretation of data<\/strong>. If leading zeros are\nremoved from an identifier, a stored key can lose its meaning. If time\nzones are ignored, events appear in the wrong order.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"the-relationship-between-data-information-and-knowledge\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The\nrelationship between data, information and knowledge<\/h3>\n<p>A data record can capture an observation. A pattern may be inferred\nfrom a series of observations. Whether that pattern constitutes a\nreliable explanation or a suitable basis for decisions must be checked\nseparately. These levels should not be equated: a statistical chart can\nbe calculated correctly and still be misleading because the data is\nincomplete.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An algorithm is a generally specified procedure<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>algorithm<\/strong> describes a task through well-defined\nsteps. The description itself is not yet a particular JavaScript,\nPureBasic or Python program. An implementation translates the procedure\ninto a specific language and runtime environment.<\/p>\n<p>Example: Find the largest value in the list\n<code>12, 18, 25, 19<\/code>.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Take the first value as the provisional maximum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compare each subsequent value with this maximum in turn.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If a value is greater, record it as the new maximum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At the end, output the recorded value.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Start: Maximum = 12\n18 &gt; 12  → Maximum = 18\n25 &gt; 18  → Maximum = 25\n19 &gt; 25  → Maximum remains 25\nResult: 25<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The strength of the rule is that it is not designed specifically for\nthese four numbers. It also works for other <strong>non-empty<\/strong>\nlists of numbers. The condition ‘non-empty’ is one of its assumptions.\nFor an empty list, one must specify whether there is no result or an\nexplicitly defined special value.<\/p>\n<p>A further point: an algorithm is usually understood as a finitely\ndescribable, step-by-step procedure with a clear goal condition.\n<strong>Not every arbitrary computer program halts for all\ninputs.<\/strong> Programs may, for example, deliberately wait\nindefinitely for events. For a specific algorithm, the inputs and the\nintended termination must therefore be specified precisely.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"correctness-and-computational-cost-are-different-questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Correctness\nand computational cost are different questions<\/h3>\n<p>The maximum-finding algorithm must satisfy two requirements: it\nshould produce the <strong>correct result<\/strong> while incurring an\nacceptable <strong>computational cost<\/strong>. For a list with a\nmillion values, the linear procedure examines every value in principle.\nThis is reasonable here if no additional prior information is\navailable.<\/p>\n<p>An already sorted list may allow a simpler query. A database with an\nindex can use different access paths. However, the index must be\ncreated, stored and updated when changes occur. <strong>Faster reads can\nmean more storage and slower writes.<\/strong> Such trade-offs are a\nrecurring topic in computer science.<\/p>\n<p>In <strong>M1-L3 – Algorithms and data structures<\/strong>, such\nmethods and their growth rates are discussed in detail. Here the basic\nidea is sufficient: we assess not only an individual program execution,\nbut also how the cost develops for larger inputs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Abstraction: Selecting the relevant part<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>abstraction<\/strong> describes a problem while omitting\ndetails that are unnecessary for the question at hand. This makes\ncomplex relationships manageable in the first place.<\/p>\n<p>For an order management system, one could initially define the\nfollowing domain model:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer (customer ID)\n   │  one-to-many\n   ▼\nOrder (order ID, date, status)\n   │  one-to-many\n   ▼\nOrder item (product, quantity, unit price)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The diagram is neither a database schema nor finished software. It\nmerely describes relationships. One can then decide how to organise this\ninformation as tables, documents or files and which rules the system\nshould enforce.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>model<\/strong> is thus a purpose-oriented representation of\nreality or of a technical process. Models are useful because they allow\nus to discuss and examine a problem before choosing a specific\nimplementation.<\/p>\n<p>However, every abstraction has limits. A model that only recognises\n‘order is paid’ may, for instance, fail when dealing with partial\npayments, refunds or simultaneous payment attempts. An abstraction\nbecomes faulty not because it is simple, but when it <strong>omits\nessential properties<\/strong> needed to make a particular statement.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"three-levels-that-should-be-distinguished\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three levels that\nshould be distinguished<\/h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Question it answers<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Domain model<\/td>\n<td>A work has several alternative titles<\/td>\n<td>What does an entity mean?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logical model<\/td>\n<td>Tables, fields and unique keys<\/td>\n<td>How is the information related?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Technical implementation<\/td>\n<td>SQLite file, PostgreSQL server, API<\/td>\n<td>What technology does the solution run on?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A good separation makes later changes possible. One can replace the\nstorage system and largely retain the domain model; however, an\nunsuitable domain model <strong>cannot<\/strong> be repaired by adding\nmore hardware.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The four traditional perspectives of computer science<\/h2>\n<p>The division into <strong>theoretical, practical, technical and\napplied computer science<\/strong> is widely used as a means of\norientation in German-speaking countries. It is not an exhaustive,\nglobally uniform list of disciplines. Modern curricula distinguish\nnumerous other areas of knowledge, such as security, human-computer\ninteraction and artificial intelligence.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"theoretical-computer-science-what-is-possible-in-principle\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Theoretical\ncomputer science – What is possible in principle?<\/h3>\n<p>This area deals with formal languages, logic, computability,\nalgorithms and their limits. One guiding question is: does a procedure\nexist for a precisely defined task that always finds the correct answer?\nAnother is: how quickly does the required computational cost grow as the\ninput becomes larger?<\/p>\n<p>Theoretical computer science is therefore not merely ‘mathematics\nwithout application’. It provides foundations for compilers,\ncryptography, databases and the assessment of algorithms.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"practical-computer-science-how-is-complex-software-developed\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical\ncomputer science – How is complex software developed?<\/h3>\n<p>It deals, among other things, with programming languages, software\nengineering, operating systems, databases and the organisation of\nsoftware systems. Typical questions are: which interfaces are reliable?\nHow are changes tested? How can concurrent access be prevented from\ndamaging data?<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"technical-computer-science-how-does-a-physical-computer-perform-computations\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Technical\ncomputer science – How does a physical computer perform\ncomputations?<\/h3>\n<p>This area brings together digital circuits, computer architecture,\nmemory, networks and embedded systems. It explains why a processor can\nexecute instructions, why memory access takes time and why a graphics\nprocessing unit may be better suited to certain tasks than a\ngeneral-purpose processor.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"applied-computer-science-which-solution-suits-a-domain-specific-task\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Applied\ncomputer science – Which solution suits a domain-specific task?<\/h3>\n<p>Here, methods are developed and adapted for specific fields:\nmedicine, media management, business, manufacturing, science, robotics\nor administration. A reliable system often requires all three of the\nother perspectives.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Theory       → Is a procedure possible, and how costly is it?\nPractice     → How can it become maintainable software?\nTechnology   → How does the underlying machine work?\nApplication  → Does the solution meet the actual need?<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>As a disciplinary reference, the joint <strong>CS2023 curriculum from\nACM, IEEE Computer Society and AAAI<\/strong> lists, among other things,\nalgorithms, computer architecture, data management, operating systems,\nsecurity, software engineering, artificial intelligence and mathematical\nand statistical foundations. A reading course can organise these topics\nin an understandable way, but it replaces neither the full scope nor the\nassessed work of an accredited degree programme.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computability is not the same as practical solvability<\/h2>\n<p>Computer science problems can be divided into three deliberately\nsimplified groups.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><p><strong>Computable and manageable in everyday practice.<\/strong>\nAdding a list of numbers or finding its maximum can be done with a\nstraightforward procedure even for large data sets.<\/p><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><p><strong>Computable in principle, but potentially very\ncostly.<\/strong> In some planning and combinatorial optimisation\nproblems, the number of possible solutions grows extremely quickly. An\nexact result may then be too costly for large inputs; approximation\nmethods or heuristics may be more practical. ‘Difficult’ does not\nautomatically mean ‘unsolvable’ here.<\/p><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><p><strong>Algorithmically undecidable in general.<\/strong> The\nclassical <strong>halting problem<\/strong> asks whether an arbitrary\nprogram eventually terminates on an arbitrary input. There is no general\nprocedure that correctly decides this question for all programs and\ninputs. For particular restricted programs, of course, it can still be\nanswered.<\/p><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This distinction is important: <strong>More processors help with some\nresource bottlenecks, but not with undecidability in principle.<\/strong>\nArtificial intelligence does not remove this limit either; it can,\nhowever, provide useful estimates or suggested solutions for many\nconcrete problems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Correctness, quality and responsibility<\/h2>\n<p>An application can run technically and still be wrong in terms of the\ndomain. Three levels help with assessment:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Domain correctness:<\/strong> Do the results correspond to\nthe agreed problem and its exceptions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Technical reliability:<\/strong> Can the system withstand\nincorrect inputs, interrupted connections and concurrent use?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Appropriate responsibility:<\/strong> Are rights, sensitive\ninformation, retention periods and the consequences of incorrect results\ntaken into account?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A program that searches local files has different risks from a\npublicly accessible service with customer accounts. An AI system that\nrecommends texts requires different safeguards from a robot that drives\na motor. These differences affect architecture and cost as early as the\nmodelling phase.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Tests<\/strong> can reveal individual errors or increase\nconfidence in an implementation. However, they do not automatically\nprove that all conceivable cases are handled correctly. Conversely,\nmathematical proofs can establish certain properties under clearly\nstated assumptions; a formal proof does not replace checking whether the\nassumptions fit the real environment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical scenarios<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-a-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario A – Local media\ncatalogue<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Initial problem:<\/strong> A person manages many files and\nwants to find duplicates and alternative titles.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Appropriate solution:<\/strong> A data model with stable\nidentifiers, a local database or initially a file, together with search\nand comparison methods. The user can check results before entries are\nmerged.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What would be oversized?<\/strong> A cloud cluster consisting\nof many servers. For a single local collection, hosting, data protection\nand maintenance costs would often outweigh the benefits.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-b-small-online-analysis-tool\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario B – Small online\nanalysis tool<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Initial problem:<\/strong> Several customers should be able to\nupload an analysis and retrieve results.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → Application/API → Processing → Database\n                   │\n                   └── Authorisation checks, logging, error handling<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The domain logic may be similar to the local version; additional\nconsiderations are permissions, shared use, failures and ongoing hosting\ncosts. <strong>API<\/strong> means <em>Application Programming\nInterface<\/em>, a defined interface between programs. Whether this\napplication is initially operated on a single server or distributed\nacross several depends on load and risk, not on a fashionable term.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-c-ai-assistance-and-distributed-processing\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario C\n– AI assistance and distributed processing<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Initial problem:<\/strong> Many documents or media files are\nto be evaluated and categorised automatically.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Appropriate solution:<\/strong> First define, from the domain\nperspective, which statements should be checked and how\nmisclassifications can be detected. Then choose an appropriate algorithm\nor model. Under heavy load, a queue can distribute jobs among several\nprocessing programs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limit:<\/strong> A powerful AI model does not necessarily\nproduce a factually correct assessment. Data provenance and\nconfidentiality, as well as opportunities for human correction, remain\nimportant. The later lessons M5-L2–M6-L6 explain how such models work\ntechnically and how they are operated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When is which level of computer science effort needed?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Usually helpful first step<\/th>\n<th>Typically unnecessary at the start<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Small one-off data conversion<\/td>\n<td>Simple procedure, plausibility check<\/td>\n<td>Distributed system<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Growing local database<\/td>\n<td>Sound data model, suitable index, backup<\/td>\n<td>Multiple database regions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public web application<\/td>\n<td>Permissions, tests, secure deployment, measurement<\/td>\n<td>Complex microservice landscape<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI processing with many jobs<\/td>\n<td>Quality metric, queue, cost limit<\/td>\n<td>Training an enormous foundation model of one’s own<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety-critical control system<\/td>\n<td>Verifiable constraints and independent protective functions<\/td>\n<td>Uncontrolled LLM access to actuators<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Sometimes the correct solution is to use <strong>less<\/strong>\ntechnology. A manageable program is often easier to check, cheaper to\nmaintain and safer to operate than an unnecessarily complex system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Computer science investigates the representation, processing and\ntransmission of information. Its tools include not only programming\nlanguages but also <strong>abstractions, models, algorithms, reasoning\nabout correctness and architectural decisions<\/strong>. The best\ntechnical solution is the one that solves the specific problem reliably\nand economically under realistic constraints.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2 – How computers really work<\/a>\nexplains the machine behind the program. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3 – Algorithms and data\nstructures<\/a> explores the choice of procedures in more depth. The\nconnections to mathematics are explained in the provisional mathematics\nbridge; specific mathematics module identifiers have not yet been\nverified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>ACM/IEEE-CS/AAAI, CS2023 – Knowledge Areas:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://csed.acm.org/knowledge-areas/\">https://csed.acm.org/knowledge-areas/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(curricular context; checked 04.11.2026).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>ACM/IEEE-CS/AAAI, CS2023 – Overview:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://csed.acm.org/\">https://csed.acm.org/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> (checked\n04.11.2026).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Further foundations in the course: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a>; the distinction between\npractical and theoretical computer science is to be understood as a\ndidactic classification, not as a complete list of disciplines.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M1-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M1-L2 – How computers really work<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: Foundations of computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Trace the path from an\nindividual bit through the operation of a processor and operating system\nto the launch of a program, understanding why computers can perform\ncalculations and what limitations their hardware imposes on desktop,\nserver and AI applications.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: From a stored file to a running application<\/h2>\n<p>From the outside, a computer appears to be a single unit: you open an\napplication, click “Search”, and shortly afterwards a result appears. In\nreality, several layers must work together. A persistently stored\nprogram is made executable with the help of the operating system; a\nprocessor handles instructions; memory keeps data available; devices\nprovide input and receive output.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Human / another program\n          │\n          ▼\nApplication (user interface, business logic)\n          │\n          ▼\nOperating system (processes, memory, files, devices)\n          │\n          ▼\nCPU ─────── Cache ─────── RAM\n │                         │\n ├── Devices / network     │\n ├── GPU (optional)         │\n └──────── SSD / other persistent storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is a <strong>functional diagram<\/strong>, not an exact circuit\ndiagram. A modern computer has multiple cores, controllers, caches and\nconnections. The following sections break the overall picture into\nunderstandable parts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bits, bytes and the meaning of encoding<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>bit<\/strong>, from <em>binary digit<\/em>, can logically\nrepresent one of two values: <code>0<\/code> or <code>1<\/code>. Eight\nbits form a <strong>byte<\/strong>. This allows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>256<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{8} = 256<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbit patterns. Whether a pattern is understood as an unsigned number,\npart of a text or something else is determined by its\n<strong>encoding<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>binary system<\/strong> uses the place values 1, 2, 4, 8,\n16, 32 and so on instead of the decimal place values 1, 10, 100.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Bit position (from the right)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Value<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">32<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">64<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">128<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The bit pattern <code>00001101<\/code>, interpreted as an\n<strong>unsigned<\/strong> binary number, means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8 + 4 + 1 = 13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the same interpretation, the pattern <code>11111111<\/code>\nrepresents <code>255<\/code>. Without further specification, eight bits\n<strong>cannot<\/strong> represent every integer. Depending on the data\ntype, negative numbers, other ranges of values or special encodings may\nalso be used.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-same-sequence-of-bytes-can-have-different-meanings\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why\nthe same sequence of bytes can have different meanings<\/h3>\n<p>A particular byte can represent a letter under a character encoding,\nan integer under a numerical encoding, or part of a colour value in an\nimage file. <strong>UTF-8<\/strong> is a widely used character encoding\nthat converts Unicode characters into one or more bytes. Thus, a\ncomputer does not inherently “know” that a sequence of bytes represents\na word: programs and file formats provide the rules for interpreting\nit.<\/p>\n<p>This is crucial in a database: a film identifier <code>000417<\/code>\nshould be retained as an identifier. If it is carelessly interpreted as\na number, the leading zeros may disappear. Even at this point, computer\narchitecture, computer science models and the data management discussed\nin M1-L5 intersect.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"when-the-range-of-values-is-insufficient\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When the range of\nvalues is insufficient<\/h3>\n<p>In an <strong>unsigned<\/strong> calculation restricted to eight bits,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200 + 100 = 300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis value cannot be represented in eight bits. If the system simply\ntruncated the result to the lowest eight bits, <code>44<\/code> would\nremain, because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>256<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>44<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300 = 256 + 44<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOther execution environments detect overflow, use larger data types or\ndefine a different behaviour. <strong>A mathematically correct result\ndoes not necessarily fit every technical data type.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logic gates: calculation through simple decisions<\/h2>\n<p>Digital electronics uses distinguishable electrical states. These are\noften simplified to <code>0<\/code> and <code>1<\/code>. This does not\nmean that “zero volts” and “a fixed voltage for one” apply everywhere:\nelectrical signal levels, noise margins and circuit families vary.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>transistor<\/strong> can, in a simplified description, be\nused as a controllable switch in digital circuits. Many transistors form\n<strong>logic gates<\/strong>, which implement specific logical\nfunctions. Three fundamental functions of <strong>Boolean\nalgebra<\/strong> are:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>AND:<\/strong> the output is <code>1<\/code> if both inputs\nare <code>1<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>OR:<\/strong> the output is <code>1<\/code> if at least one\ninput is <code>1<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NOT:<\/strong> a single input is logically inverted.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>A<\/th>\n<th>B<\/th>\n<th>A AND B<\/th>\n<th>A OR B<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>There is also the <strong>XOR<\/strong> function (<em>exclusive\nOR<\/em>): it produces <code>1<\/code> if exactly one of the two inputs is\n<code>1<\/code>. XOR and AND can be used to construct a simple\n<strong>half adder<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Inputs A, B\n     │\n     ├── XOR ──► Sum bit\n     └── AND ──► Carry bit\n\nExample: 1 + 1 → Sum bit 0, carry 1\n                 Result 10₂ = 2₁₀<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Multiple adders, memory components and control logic enable more\ncomplex computational steps. This is the transition from individual\nlogical rules to a machine capable of executing instructions.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"computational-logic-and-stored-state\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computational logic and\nstored state<\/h3>\n<p>In the basic model, a gate responds only to its current inputs.\nHowever, a machine must also be able to <strong>remember<\/strong>\nsomething. For this purpose, digital electronics uses circuits with\nstate, such as flip-flops; these can be used to build registers and\nother memory structures. A computer continuously combines\n<strong>combinational logic<\/strong> (computation) and <strong>storage\nlogic<\/strong> (retaining state).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The most important hardware components<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_002_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Schematic comparison of an SSD as persistent storage, RAM as volatile working memory and the CPU as a processing unit, with data paths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic comparison of an SSD as\npersistent storage, RAM as volatile working memory and the CPU as a\nprocessing unit, with data paths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: The arrows show typical logical operations. The actual\npath through the operating system, memory controller, cache and\ninput/output is more complex; the CPU, RAM and SSD do not communicate\nvia direct point-to-point connections in every architecture.<\/em><\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component<\/th>\n<th>Full name / function<\/th>\n<th>Key point<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CPU<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><em>Central Processing Unit<\/em>, central processor<\/td>\n<td>Executes general machine instructions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RAM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><em>Random Access Memory<\/em>, working memory<\/td>\n<td>Keeps currently required data and code available<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SSD<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><em>Solid State Drive<\/em>, flash-based mass storage<\/td>\n<td>Normally retains files without power<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GPU<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><em>Graphics Processing Unit<\/em>, graphics/parallel processor<\/td>\n<td>Accelerates suitable large numbers of similar computational\noperations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cache<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Fast intermediate memory<\/td>\n<td>Avoids some accesses to slower memory<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Other components include the motherboard, controllers, network\ninterfaces, power supply and interconnections. In computer architecture,\nthe word <strong>bus<\/strong> describes a transmission path or system\nfor exchanging data and control signals. Modern computers have various\npoint-to-point connections and bus structures rather than a single\nconnection through which everything must pass.<\/p>\n<p>A computer is not powerful simply because its CPU is fast. Data must\nbe available in time. A fast CPU may wait for data, a GPU may remain\nunused because of insufficient memory, and a server may be slowed down\nby slow storage access or network traffic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What the CPU does when executing an instruction<\/h2>\n<p>A processor executes <strong>machine instructions<\/strong>, for\nexample to load, add or compare values, store results or jump to another\nlocation in the program.<\/p>\n<p>The simplified basic principle is often called\n<strong>Fetch–Decode–Execute<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Fetch the machine instruction\n             │\n             ▼\nDecode the instruction\n             │\n             ▼\nExecute the operation\n             │\n             ▼\nMake the result available → further instructions<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Registers<\/strong> are small, very fast storage areas\ndirectly within the processor. An <strong>arithmetic logic unit<\/strong>\n(<strong>ALU<\/strong>) performs operations including addition and\nlogical operations. The control logic coordinates which steps take\nplace. For example, addition requires operands to be available in a\nsuitable form; the result is stored in a register or later at another\nlocation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"instruction-set-and-specific-processor-design\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Instruction set\nand specific processor design<\/h3>\n<p>An <strong>instruction set architecture<\/strong>\n(<strong>ISA<\/strong>) describes the contract visible to programs: which\ninstructions, registers and rules a processor provides. Examples include\n<strong>x86-64<\/strong> and various <strong>Arm<\/strong> architectures.\nA specific <strong>microarchitecture<\/strong>, by contrast, determines\n<em>how<\/em> a particular processor fulfils this contract\ninternally.<\/p>\n<p>Two processors can therefore understand the same instruction set but\ndiffer in speed, energy efficiency or cost. Modern processors can\noverlap operations in <strong>pipelines<\/strong>, prepare some\ninstructions <strong>out of their original order<\/strong> and operate in\nparallel across several <strong>cores<\/strong>. Nevertheless, the simple\nfetch–decode–execute sequence remains a useful conceptual model.<\/p>\n<p>A clock frequency of three gigahertz, for example, means, in\nsimplified terms, three billion clock cycles per second –\n<strong>not<\/strong> three billion completed lines of program code. The\namount of work that can be completed per cycle varies depending on the\nprocessor, instruction and operating state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Memory hierarchy: why data paths matter so much<\/h2>\n<p>The processor needs data, but not all kinds of memory are equally\nfast, large or expensive. Several levels are therefore combined:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>CPU registers        very close, very small\n      ▲\nCPU caches (L1/L2/L3) fast, limited capacity\n      ▲\nRAM                  larger, normally volatile\n      ▲\nSSD / mass storage   persistent, slower for many accesses\n      ▲\nBackup / archive     additional, separately protected copy<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The arrows do not represent a mandatory physical series connection.\nInstead, the diagram shows a typical hierarchy of access types. A\n<strong>cache<\/strong> stores frequently or predictably needed\ninformation close to the processing unit. If the working data set fits\nwell in a cache, a program can run quickly. If significantly slower\nmemory must constantly be accessed, the situation changes.<\/p>\n<p>With conventional technology, <strong>RAM<\/strong> loses its contents\nwithout power. <strong>SSDs<\/strong> use non-volatile memory and\nnormally retain data without power. Nevertheless, an SSD is not\nautomatically a backup: a file deleted by mistake, a faulty drive or\nmalicious software can destroy data there as well. <strong>Data\nstorage<\/strong>, <strong>data durability<\/strong> and\n<strong>recoverability<\/strong> are different properties.<\/p>\n<p>Consider a media management application: the large video file is on\nthe SSD; ideally, a search requires only its small metadata. A search\nindex can prevent the program from having to search every video again.\nThis is why data structure and memory access strategy may be more\nimportant than an even faster CPU.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"do-not-confuse-ram-paging-files-and-virtual-memory\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Do not\nconfuse RAM, paging files and virtual memory<\/h3>\n<p>The operating system usually provides a process with a\n<strong>virtual address space<\/strong>. The program uses virtual\naddresses; the hardware and operating system map these to suitable\nphysical memory areas. This protects processes from one another and\nfacilitates memory management.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Virtual memory does not automatically mean paging to an\nSSD.<\/strong> Many virtual memory pages reside in RAM or are mapped to\nfiles on demand. Only under certain circumstances can suitable memory\npages be moved to mass storage via a paging file or swap partition.\nFrequently loading data from slower storage can severely degrade\nperformance. Additional swap space is therefore not necessarily a\nsubstitute for insufficient RAM.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>CPU and GPU: different kinds of work<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>CPU<\/strong> is suited to diverse program logic involving\nmany decisions and changing tasks. A <strong>GPU<\/strong> has a large\nnumber of computing units particularly well suited to processing\nrelated, data-parallel operations. In addition to graphics, GPU hardware\nis therefore used for certain matrix calculations in artificial\nintelligence.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>CPU:  Decision logic → many different operations\nGPU:  Large data sets → many similar partial calculations in parallel<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is not an absolute distinction: CPUs are also parallel, and GPUs\ncan execute complex programs. Not every algorithm can be parallelised\neffectively. If small amounts of data first have to be transferred\nbetween main memory and graphics memory at considerable cost, a GPU may\nbe a poor choice in a particular case, despite its higher theoretical\ncomputing power.<\/p>\n<p><strong>VRAM<\/strong> (<em>Video Random Access Memory<\/em>) is the\neveryday term for graphics memory. For large language models, it can\nbecome a limiting factor. Model weights themselves, intermediate\nresults, context and concurrent requests require memory – a relationship\nexplained in more detail in <strong>M6-L6 – AI infrastructure and\ndistributed computing<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From source code to an executable instruction<\/h2>\n<p>A processor does not directly understand the syntax of a high-level\nprogramming language. A <strong>compiler<\/strong> translates program\ntext into another form, often ahead of time into machine instructions or\nvia intermediate stages. <strong>Assembly language<\/strong> is a\nmachine-oriented textual representation of instructions; the term\n<strong>assembler<\/strong> refers to the tool that translates it and is\nalso used to refer to the language itself.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>interpreter<\/strong> processes a program representation\nwith the help of an execution environment. Some systems combine both\ntechniques. <strong>JIT<\/strong> stands for <em>Just-in-Time<\/em>\ncompilation: suitable code is translated into machine code only at run\ntime, often to speed up repeated execution.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>PureBasic source code → Compiler → Executable file → CPU\n\nJavaScript text       → JavaScript engine\n                      → Interpretation and/or JIT compilation\n                      → Executable operations on the CPU<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>These examples are simplified, typical execution paths, not mandatory\nrules for every possible implementation. The <strong>runtime\nenvironment<\/strong> often provides additional functions, such as memory\nmanagement, standard libraries, error handling and access to specific\ninterfaces.<\/p>\n<p>A machine-code program is also tied to the agreed <strong>processor\narchitecture<\/strong> and frequently to a specific operating system\nformat. The same source code may have to be compiled again for different\ntarget systems. “It runs on my computer” therefore does not\nautomatically mean “it runs unchanged on every server”.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What the operating system manages<\/h2>\n<p>Windows, Linux and macOS handle tasks including process management,\nmemory management, file access, device control, user permissions and\nnetwork communication. The <strong>kernel<\/strong> is the particularly\nprivileged core of the operating system, which manages fundamental\nresources and provides interfaces to programs.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>program<\/strong> is initially a stored description of\ninstructions. A <strong>process<\/strong> is a running instance of a\nprogram with allocated resources and normally its own virtual address\nspace. A process can contain multiple <strong>threads<\/strong> –\nexecution flows. They share key resources but have their own execution\nstates.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>             Operating system / scheduler\n                       │\n             ┌─────────┼─────────┐\n             │         │         │\n          Browser   Database   Media tool\n          process   process    process\n             │\n        multiple threads possible<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>scheduler<\/strong> determines when execution flows receive\ncomputing time. <strong>Concurrency<\/strong> means that multiple tasks\nmake progress over overlapping periods; <strong>parallelism<\/strong>\nmeans that they actually run at the same time. A computer with one core\ncan work on tasks concurrently by switching between them without\nexecuting them in parallel at the same instant.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"concurrent-changes-need-rules\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concurrent changes need\nrules<\/h3>\n<p>Two threads read the same counter with a value of <code>0<\/code>.\nBoth want to increment it. If both first read <code>0<\/code>,\nindependently calculate <code>1<\/code> and then write back\n<code>1<\/code>, the result is <code>1<\/code> rather than <code>2<\/code>.\nThis is a problem known as a <strong>race condition<\/strong>: the result\ndepends on the temporal order of events.<\/p>\n<p>Such situations may require <strong>synchronisation<\/strong>, for\nexample suitable locks or atomic operations. In a single-user\napplication this may be easy to manage; in an online service with many\nconcurrent changes, it becomes considerably more important. Later\nlessons M1-L7 and M3-L5 discuss the details.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Devices, input and output, interrupts<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Input/output<\/strong> (<strong>I/O<\/strong>) encompasses data\nexchange with SSDs, keyboards, cameras, network devices and many other\ncomponents. Applications normally use operating system functions for\nthis rather than controlling the electronics of each device\ndirectly.<\/p>\n<p>A device or controller can request attention through an\n<strong>interrupt<\/strong>. The CPU does not therefore have to keep\nchecking by itself whether, for example, new input has arrived. For\nlarger data transfers, specialised mechanisms such as <strong>Direct\nMemory Access<\/strong> (<strong>DMA<\/strong>) can enable data movements\nwithout requiring the CPU to copy every single byte itself. This does\nnot mean that the CPU no longer performs any organisational work in the\nprocess.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application → Operating system interface → Device driver\n                                          │\n                                          ▼\n                                    Device controller\n                                          │\n                                          ▼\n                                       SSD / network<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Slow file or network access is therefore not necessarily slow\ncomputation. The process may be waiting for an external response. This\nis an important distinction in the later performance analysis of\nservers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>File systems and persistent data<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>file system<\/strong> organises files, directories, access\npermissions, metadata and the allocation of content to storage areas.\nExamples include <strong>NTFS<\/strong> on Windows, <strong>ext4<\/strong>\non Linux and <strong>APFS<\/strong> on macOS. Depending on its\nconfiguration, a particular operating system can also use other file\nsystems.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application → Operating system → File system → SSD<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The application knows a file name. The file system manages where the\nassociated content is stored and how it can be found again. File systems\nmay have mechanisms for limiting certain structural errors after a\ncrash. However, they do not automatically guarantee that a change\ncurrently being made by the application has been written completely and\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<p>A database extends simple file storage with queries, consistency\nrules and often transactions. A <strong>backup<\/strong>, in turn,\ncreates a separate copy in case the original data is lost. Details\nfollow in M1-L5 and M3-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Launching a program – the complete sequence<\/h2>\n<p>When an application is launched, the following happens in simplified\nterms:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The operating system receives the request to start an executable\nprogram file.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It checks basic access and execution conditions and establishes a\nprocess.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Required code and data are mapped into the virtual address space;\nnot everything has to be copied immediately and completely from the SSD\ninto RAM.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The system allocates execution time. The CPU starts processing the\nappropriate machine instructions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">As required, the program requests files, graphics rendering, network\nmessages or other services.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When the program terminates, resources are released again;\ntypically, only explicitly saved or otherwise persisted data\nremains.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Program file on SSD\n         │\n         ▼\nOperating system loader → Process / virtual address space\n         │\n         ▼\nScheduler → CPU processes instructions\n         │\n         ├── Data from cache and RAM\n         ├── File/network access via operating system\n         └── GPU for suitable tasks\n         │\n         ▼\nOutput / persistent storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This completes the journey from the bit pattern to the actual\napplication: the layers are different, but they continually\ninteract.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical examples: how hardware knowledge changes\ndecisions<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-a-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario A – Local media\ncatalogue<\/h3>\n<p>A local application manages film titles and file paths. The database\nquery is slow. A more expensive processor might help, but first it is\nworth determining whether every search reads the entire data set again.\nA suitable <strong>index<\/strong> can make a much greater difference\nthan additional CPU power. Frequent SSD access or the unnecessary\nloading of large preview images may also become a bottleneck.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful operational question:<\/strong> How can the archive be\nprotected against failure, operator error and faulty imports? For a\nlocal application, a simple backup strategy may be more important than a\nserver architecture.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-b-small-public-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario B – Small public\nservice<\/h3>\n<p>Several users send requests at the same time. Here, processes,\nthreads, RAM, the network and storage have to be considered together. If\na slow-responding database causes the delay, a more powerful web server\nCPU may not solve the problem.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ──Network──► Web application ──► Database\n                        │                   │\n                       CPU/RAM          Index/SSD/RAM\n                        └── Metrics + logs ─┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Operational implications:<\/strong> Updates, user permissions,\nmonitoring, backups, encryption, data protection and ongoing costs are\nall part of the picture. A straightforward application on a single\nrented server may initially be more appropriate than a complicated\ncluster.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-c-processing-images-and-ai\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario C – Processing\nimages and AI<\/h3>\n<p>An image or AI application may use a GPU. Whether this helps depends\non the potential for parallelisation, the model size and memory paths. A\ngraphics card with high computing power but insufficient graphics memory\nmay be unsuitable for a particular model. If image files are first\nloaded slowly from storage or the network, additional GPU power may be\nof little benefit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational implications:<\/strong> Power demand, waste heat,\nmemory capacity, software compatibility, computing time and, for cloud\nGPUs, ongoing rental costs. For infrequent tasks, using a remote service\nmay be more appropriate; for confidential data, local operation may\noffer advantages but creates maintenance obligations of its own.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common misconceptions<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assumption<\/th>\n<th>More technically precise explanation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“Twice the clock frequency means twice the speed.”<\/td>\n<td>Instructions per cycle, cores, memory and workload affect\nperformance.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Virtual memory is simply storage space on an SSD.”<\/td>\n<td>Virtual addresses are an addressing and protection concept; paging\nis only one possible mechanism.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“More threads make every program faster.”<\/td>\n<td>Additional threads may compete for resources and require\nsynchronisation.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“The SSD is persistent, so I do not need a backup.”<\/td>\n<td>Persistence during power loss does not protect against deletion,\nfailure or attacks.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“A GPU is fundamentally faster than a CPU.”<\/td>\n<td>The advantage depends on the type of task, data movement, memory and\nsoftware.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“A server is a completely different kind of computer.”<\/td>\n<td>A server primarily denotes a role; hardware and operation may be\ndesigned at a different scale.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Electronic circuits represent logical states as bits. Logic gates,\nregisters and further components form processors that execute specified\nmachine instructions. Operating systems coordinate processes, threads,\nfiles, memory and devices. Cache, RAM, SSDs, CPUs and GPUs differ in\ntheir properties and tasks. These differences lead to performance limits\nas well as architectural decisions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3 – Algorithms and data\nstructures<\/a> explores the selection of efficient methods in greater\ndepth. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7 – Further areas of\ncomputer science<\/a> explains further aspects of concurrency and\ncompilers. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1 – From PCs to\nservers<\/a> builds directly on the hardware and operating system\nfoundations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Arm Developer – Introducing the Arm architecture:<\/strong>\n<a\nhref=\"https://developer.arm.com/-/media/Arm%20Developer%20Community/PDF/Learn%20the%20Architecture/Introducing%20the%20Arm%20architecture.pdf?revision=0b6b67ea-edd7-4975-b23c-d4904ac0cb35\">https://developer.arm.com/-/media/Arm%20Developer%20Community/PDF/Learn%20the%20Architecture/Introducing%20the%20Arm%20architecture.pdf?revision=0b6b67ea-edd7-4975-b23c-d4904ac0cb35<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(instruction set, registers, software/hardware contract; reviewed\n04.11.2026).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Linux kernel documentation – Memory Management:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/index.html\">https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/index.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(virtual memory, demand paging, processes; reviewed 04.11.2026).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Mozilla Developer Network – JavaScript Engine:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Engine/JavaScript\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Engine/JavaScript<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(interpretation and JIT compilation; reviewed 04.11.2026).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>ACM/IEEE-CS/AAAI – CS2023 Computer Architecture and\nOrganization:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://csed.acm.org/knowledge-areas/\">https://csed.acm.org/knowledge-areas/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(academic context; reviewed 04.11.2026).<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M1-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M1-L3 – Algorithms and data structures<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: Foundations of computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4 – Software engineering and\nsystem design<\/a> · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L4 –\nAlgorithms and data structures in depth<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Why the right algorithm can be more important than\na fast processor<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a program manages a collection of files. It needs to find the\nentry with a particular identifier. With 20 entries, the search method\nhardly matters. With ten million entries, the same decision about how to\norganise the data can make the difference between an almost immediate\nresponse and many unnecessary comparisons.<\/p>\n<p>Computer science therefore examines not only <em>how<\/em> a program\nis written, but also <strong>which procedure<\/strong> solves a problem\nand <strong>how<\/strong> the data are made available for that\npurpose.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An <strong>algorithm<\/strong> is a precisely described, finite\nsequence of steps for solving a class of tasks.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A <strong>data structure<\/strong> is an organised way of storing and\naccessing data.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Complexity analysis<\/strong> considers how much time and\nmemory a procedure requires depending on the size of the input.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A good data structure makes certain algorithms efficient. A good\nalgorithm takes advantage of the properties of the data structure. The\ntwo decisions belong together.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Request: &quot;Find customer 4711&quot;\n              │\n              ▼\n     Algorithm (search rule)\n              │\n              ▼\n  Data structure (e.g. list,\n       hash table, tree)\n              │\n              ▼\n       Result / not found<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Initial problem:<\/strong> Without suitable organisation, the\napplication may have to examine many irrelevant records. <strong>When is\nit unnecessary?<\/strong> For a small list that is rarely searched, a\ncomplicated structure is often more costly to develop and maintain than\na simple loop.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Searching: From sequential scanning to targeted access<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"11-lineare-suche\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Linear search<\/h3>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_003_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Comparison of linear and binary searches for the value 41 in the same sorted sequence of numbers: seven checks versus three checks.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of linear and binary searches\nfor the value 41 in the same sorted sequence of numbers: seven checks\nversus three checks.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustration: Teaching example for these eight values and the\nsearch value 41. The number of comparisons depends on the position; the\nworst-case estimates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napply under the respective assumptions. Binary search requires a\nsuitably sorted sequence.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Linear search<\/strong> checks one entry after another until\nthe desired value is found or all entries have been checked. It works\neven if the data are completely unordered. Its advantage is simplicity;\nit requires no special search index. Its disadvantage: in the worst\ncase, it has to check every entry.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>[Anna] [Berta] [Cem] [Dora] [Emil]\n  ↓       ↓      ↓      ↓\n     Search for &quot;Dora&quot;<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>With <code>n<\/code> entries, approximately <code>n<\/code> comparisons\nare required in the worst case:\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nread as “big O of n”.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"12-binaere-suche\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Binary search<\/h3>\n<p>If the data are <strong>sorted<\/strong> and middle elements can be\naccessed directly, half of the remaining search range can be excluded at\neach step. We first compare with the middle entry and then continue the\nsearch only to the left or only to the right. This process is repeated\nuntil the entry is found or the search range is empty.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Sorted identifiers:  [ 2  5  8  12  17  21  28  35 ]\n                              │\n                       Compare middle\n                        /           \\\n                 smaller values   larger values\n                   │                 │\n                       halve again<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>For around one million entries, at most roughly 20 such halving steps\nare needed, provided we can access every position directly. The growth\nis\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nmore precisely usually with base 2 in this case. The base does not\nmatter for the broad big-O classification.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important limitation:<\/strong> The data must already be\nsorted or must be sorted first. A sorted structure built once can\naccelerate many subsequent searches; if changes arrive constantly,\nmaintaining it can be a significant cost. In a singly linked list,\naccessing the middle element is not readily achievable at high speed –\nbinary search is therefore not automatically a sensible choice even if\nthe values are sorted.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"13-suche-per-hash-tabelle\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Searching with a hash table<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>hash function<\/strong> calculates a value from a search key\nthat is used to select a storage area. Rather than traversing the list\nfrom the beginning, the application can go directly to the relevant\narea. This works particularly well when the exact identifier is known,\nsuch as a customer ID.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer ID &quot;4711&quot;\n       │\n       ▼\n  Hash function\n       │\n       ▼\n    Bucket 42 ──► [Entry with ID 4711]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Under usual conditions, a well-organised hash table performs\nsearches, insertions and deletions with <strong>expected\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\ncost, meaning that the effort does not increase in proportion to the\ntotal number of entries. This is <strong>not<\/strong> an absolute\nguarantee: different keys may be assigned to the same area\n(<strong>collision<\/strong>). An unsuitable hash function, a poor load\nfactor or deliberately chosen inputs can significantly slow access.\nMoreover, a conventional hash table does not automatically keep its keys\nin sorted order.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Key point:<\/strong> A hash table can be ideal for exact key\nlookups; for ordered ranges such as “all orders between two dates”, a\nsearch tree or database index is often more suitable.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sorting: Why the method matters for large data sets<\/h2>\n<p>Sorting means arranging entries in a specified order, for example by\nname, date or amount. The ordering can simplify subsequent searching,\nfiltering and analysis. Simple sorting procedures compare and move\nelements repeatedly; more powerful procedures divide the task into\nsmaller parts or use additional auxiliary structures.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"three-typical-strategies\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three typical strategies<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Insertion Sort:<\/strong> Take one value at a time and insert\nit into the already sorted part. This can work very well for small or\nnearly sorted data sets. In the worst case, however, approximately a\nquadratic number of moves and comparisons is required.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Merge Sort:<\/strong> The input is repeatedly divided into\nsmaller parts. The sorted parts are then merged in a controlled manner.\nThe running time is typically\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\neven in the worst case; the usual array implementation requires\nadditional memory of order\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Quicksort:<\/strong> A selected comparison value\n(<em>pivot<\/em>) separates the data into regions that are sorted\nrecursively. On average, standard comparison-based variants are very\nfast and require\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\ntime. With an unfavourable partition, however, a simple variant can\ndeteriorate to\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nGood implementations use safeguards or change strategy when\nnecessary.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Procedure<\/th>\n<th>Typical or guaranteed time cost<\/th>\n<th>Strength<\/th>\n<th>Limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Insertion Sort<\/td>\n<td>Worst case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Simple; small or nearly sorted data sets<\/td>\n<td>Large, unfavourably ordered data sets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Merge Sort<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nincluding the worst case<\/td>\n<td>Reliably predictable<\/td>\n<td>Additional memory in the common array implementation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quicksort<\/td>\n<td>Average\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Often fast in practice<\/td>\n<td>Worst case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor simple variants<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Sort stability<\/strong> is another concept: a stable sort\npreserves the original order of elements with equal sort keys. This can\nbe important, for example, when sorting first by name and then stably by\nplace of residence. A blanket statement that “algorithm X is always the\nfastest” would be false – data patterns, memory usage and the\nimplementation also matter.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-process-of-a-merge-sort\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-process of a merge sort<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>[8, 3, 7, 1]\n     │ divide\n[8, 3]     [7, 1]\n  │           │\n[3, 8]     [1, 7]\n     │ merge\n [1, 3, 7, 8]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> If only a few values are\ntraversed once, sorting them beforehand may cost more than the\nsubsequent saving. A database index is not free either: it takes up\nstorage space and must be maintained whenever changes occur.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data structures: Not everything is a list<\/h2>\n<p>The right structure depends on <em>which operation is performed\nfrequently<\/em>. A queue requires different properties from a customer\nsearch or a representation of a road network.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"31-array-feld\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Array<\/h3>\n<p>An <strong>array<\/strong> stores elements in an ordered sequence,\nusually allowing fast access by position number. In a conventional\ncontiguous array, access to a known element is\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nInsertion in the middle of a densely populated array, on the other hand,\nmay require shifting many subsequent elements, giving\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Index:  0     1     2     3\nValue: [12]  [18]  [25]  [19]\n                    ▲\n               direct access<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Dynamic arrays can grow; occasionally they have to request a larger\nmemory area and copy values. Individual expansions are more expensive,\nbut with common strategies, repeated appending often has an\n<strong>amortised\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\ncost. This means constant cost averaged over many append operations, not\nguaranteed constant cost every time.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"32-verkettete-liste\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Linked list<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>linked list<\/strong> consists of elements that contain\nreferences to their successors (and sometimes predecessors). Inserting\nan element at the beginning does not require shifting all subsequent\nelements. However, an arbitrary entry cannot be accessed directly by its\nposition number; normally, the references are followed in sequence.\nReferences also require additional memory and often make efficient use\nof modern processor caches more difficult.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>[Anna | →] ─► [Berta | →] ─► [Cem | End]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Useful:<\/strong> When linking or removing elements at known\npositions is frequent. <strong>Over-engineered:<\/strong> When managing\nsimple sequential data with frequent positional access – an array is\noften more practical here.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"33-stack-stapel-und-queue-warteschlange\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stack and queue<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>stack<\/strong> operates according to <strong>LIFO (Last In,\nFirst Out)<\/strong>: the most recently stored entry is removed first.\nTypical examples are managing nested function calls or an undo\nfunction.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>queue<\/strong> operates according to <strong>FIFO (First\nIn, First Out)<\/strong>: the first entry to arrive is processed first.\nTypical examples are print jobs, tasks for background processes or the\norderly processing of messages.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Stack:  in → [A, B, C] → out: C first\nQueue:  in → [A, B, C] → out: A first<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Both are initially abstract access rules. They can be implemented\nusing arrays, linked lists or specialised structures. We will encounter\nqueues again later in the context of server workers and distributed\napplications.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"34-baeume\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Trees<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>tree<\/strong> arranges elements hierarchically: a root node\nhas children, which in turn may have children of their own. A\n<strong>binary search tree<\/strong> organises keys such that smaller\nvalues lie to the left and larger values to the right (with appropriate\nhandling of equal values). A search can therefore exclude a subtree at\neach step.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>             [20]\n            /    \\\n         [10]    [30]\n         /  \\      \\\n       [5] [15]    [40]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If the tree remains <strong>balanced<\/strong>, searching, insertion\nand deletion are\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nin common balanced search trees. A poorly grown, unbalanced tree can\ninstead become like a long list and require\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nin the worst case.<\/p>\n<p>Databases often use variants of <strong>B-trees/B+-trees<\/strong> as\nindices: their nodes hold multiple keys, which is particularly suitable\nfor block-oriented storage access. The particular database type and its\nindex implementation are to be distinguished in lessons M1-L5 and\nM3-L1.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"35-graphen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graphs<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>graph<\/strong> describes a set of <strong>nodes\n(vertices)<\/strong> and <strong>edges<\/strong>, that is, relationships\nbetween those nodes. Unlike a tree, a graph can have arbitrary branches,\ncycles and multiple connections. Road networks, social relationships,\ndependencies between software modules and communication networks can all\nbe modelled in this way.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>    [A] ───── [B]\n     │       / │\n     │      /  │\n    [C] ───── [D]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Breadth-first search<\/strong> (BFS) explores a graph step by\nstep according to distance measured in edges. In an unweighted graph, it\nfinds a path with the fewest possible edges. <strong>Depth-first\nsearch<\/strong> (DFS), by contrast, follows a path as deeply as possible\nbefore returning. When the graph is represented using adjacency lists,\nboth require\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(V+E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nin the usual model, with <code>V<\/code> denoting the number of vertices\nand <code>E<\/code> the number of edges.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> The “shortest path” depends on what\n<em>short<\/em> means. If roads have different lengths or costs, a simple\nbreadth-first search is insufficient; weighted pathfinding algorithms\nare then used.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three important design strategies<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"41-rekursion-ein-problem-mit-sich-selbst-beschreiben\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursion:\nDescribing a problem in terms of itself<\/h3>\n<p>A function can tackle the same type of problem again for a smaller\npart. This principle is called <strong>recursion<\/strong>. A\n<strong>base case<\/strong> is essential so that the calls eventually\nterminate. Directory trees, nested documents and tree structures can\noften be processed particularly clearly in this way.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Process folder\n  ├─ Process file\n  └─ Subfolder found\n        └─ Process subfolder<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Limitations:<\/strong> Each recursive call typically requires\nbookkeeping information on the call stack. Very deep recursion can cause\na stack overflow. An iterative solution with its own work list may be\nmore robust.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"42-dynamische-programmierung-ergebnisse-nicht-immer-neu-berechnen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamic\nprogramming: Avoiding repeated computation of results<\/h3>\n<p>Some tasks contain <strong>overlapping subproblems<\/strong>. When the\nsame subproblem occurs repeatedly, its result can be stored and reused.\nThis principle is called <strong>dynamic programming<\/strong>. Results\ncan be retained during a recursive calculation\n(<strong>memoisation<\/strong>), or the subproblems can be built up in a\nsuitable order (<strong>tabulation<\/strong>).<\/p>\n<p>A well-known example is calculating a sequence whose new value is\nderived from earlier values. Without storing intermediate results, naive\nrecursive procedures can repeat identical subproblems very many times.\nBy storing the results, each required intermediate value is calculated\nonly once.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Solve subproblem ─► Store result\n      ▲                     │\n      └──── Reuse it later ──┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> Additional memory is often required,\nand the subproblems that can actually be reused must be identified. For\na simple one-off result, this technique may be unnecessary.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"43-greedy-algorithmen-jetzt-die-beste-lokale-wahl-treffen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Greedy\nalgorithms: Making the best local choice now<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>greedy algorithm<\/strong> chooses an apparently favourable\nlocal option at each step and does not normally revise it\nretrospectively. This can be simple and fast. For some problems, an\nappropriately chosen local rule is guaranteed to lead to the globally\nbest result.<\/p>\n<p>Example: If the goal is to select as many <strong>equally valuable,\nnon-overlapping appointments<\/strong> as possible from a schedule, the\noptimal choice at each step is the still feasible appointment that ends\nearliest. For other objectives, such as appointments of differing\nimportance with different weights, the same rule is not necessarily\nsufficient.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Key point:<\/strong> Greedy is an approach, not a proof of an\noptimal result. Whether the local decision is globally correct depends\non the problem.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does big-O notation really mean?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Landau notation, or big-O notation<\/strong>, describes an\nupper bound on the asymptotic growth of cost for large inputs. In\nsimplified terms, we ask: <em>How rapidly does the work increase as the\namount of data <code>n<\/code> increases?<\/em> Constant factors and\nsmaller growth terms are omitted for this broad classification.\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nis therefore not a measured time and does not automatically describe the\naverage case exactly.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Notation<\/th>\n<th>Intuitive growth<\/th>\n<th>Typical example under suitable assumptions<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Bounded independently of <code>n<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Array access by known index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Grows very slowly<\/td>\n<td>Binary search in a sorted array<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Proportional to <code>n<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Traversing an entire list once<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Somewhat faster than linear growth<\/td>\n<td>Merge Sort<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Quadratic<\/td>\n<td>Comparing all pairs in a list<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(2^n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Exponential<\/td>\n<td>Naive exhaustive checking of many subsets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-vergleich-1000-gegenueber-1000000-datensaetzen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-comparison:\n1,000 versus 1,000,000 records<\/h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Growth<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1\\,000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1\\,000\\,000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>n<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000,000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000,000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000,000,000,000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These figures are <strong>model quantities, not values in\nmilliseconds or measurements of a particular program<\/strong>.\nNevertheless, they make the difference in magnitude visible: a quadratic\nprocedure quickly becomes problematic with very large inputs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further important distinctions:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Best Case / Average Case / Worst Case:<\/strong> the most\nfavourable, average and least favourable cases can differ\nconsiderably.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Time complexity / space complexity:<\/strong> a faster\nprocedure may require more working memory.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Amortised cost:<\/strong> expensive individual steps may be\ninexpensive on average over long sequences of operations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Constants still matter in practice:<\/strong> for small data\nsets, a procedure that scales worse in theory may be faster because of\nlow fixed costs.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Other mathematical notations are <strong>Omega (Ω)<\/strong> for an\nasymptotic lower bound and <strong>Theta (Θ)<\/strong> for a matching\nupper <em>and<\/em> lower bound. A statement such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^{2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninitially means only that, under the stated assumptions, the cost being\nconsidered <strong>grows no faster than a quadratic upper\nbound<\/strong>. It does not prove that the algorithm actually requires\nquadratic time; for example, a linear running time also belongs to the\nclass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^{2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is <code>Θ(n)<\/code> that describes a matching linear order of growth\nin the asymptotic sense. Similarly, <strong>expected cost<\/strong>\n(under a specified random or input model) and <strong>amortised\ncost<\/strong> (over a sequence of operations) are different statements –\nneither is automatically a worst-case guarantee. These distinctions are\nexplored formally in <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of computability and difficult optimisation<\/h2>\n<p>Not every clearly formulated problem can be decided by a general\nalgorithm for all conceivable inputs. A classic example is the\n<strong>halting problem<\/strong>: there is no general procedure that\nreliably decides for every arbitrary program and input whether that\nprogram will eventually halt. This is a <strong>fundamental limit of\ncomputability<\/strong>, not a question of more expensive hardware.<\/p>\n<p>We must distinguish this from <strong>difficult but computable\nproblems<\/strong>. In many optimisation tasks, we can check each\nindividual proposed solution, but to guarantee the best solution we may\nhave to consider an enormous number of possibilities. Such problems\noccur, for example, in route planning, schedule optimisation and\ncombining numerous constraints.<\/p>\n<p>An important class is called <strong>NP-hard<\/strong>. This is a term\nfrom complexity theory and is not synonymous with “unsolvable”. For some\nof these problems, practitioners use heuristics, approximation methods\nor special properties of the input data. A <strong>heuristic<\/strong> is\na practical rule that often produces useful results without guaranteeing\na perfect solution under all conditions. The precise distinction between\nNP, NP-complete and NP-hard is reserved for a later optional theoretical\nexcursion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it relevant?<\/strong> When a service needs not only\nto search for data, but to derive an <em>optimal<\/em> plan from millions\nof combinations. More cloud servers may accelerate computation, but do\nnot automatically change the fundamental difficulty of the problem.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connections to your own software ideas and later cloud\narchitecture<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a locally developed tool that manages video metadata, file\ndirectories or customer identifiers. For a few hundred entries, a simple\ntable is sufficient. As the collection grows, we should first examine\nthe actual operation: are we searching for an identifier, sorting\nchronologically, filtering by category or traversing a network of\nrelationships? Only then can a suitable data structure be chosen.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A – Local media\ncatalogue<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Requested video ID\n       │\n       ▼\nIndex / hash table ──► Record\n       │\n       └─ instead of reading every video individually<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An ordered index can also be useful when sorting by publication date\nor searching for a date range. However, indices require disk space and\nmust be updated when records are added or changed.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-small-online-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B – Small online\nservice<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → Application → Database query\n                             │\n                         Use index?\n                             │\n                       Return matches<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In an SQL database (Structured Query Language = language for\nrelational data queries), the <strong>query plan<\/strong>, for example,\ncan determine whether an existing index is used or an entire table has\nto be read. The official SQLite documentation explains how indices can\naccelerate both search and sorting operations and how a cost-based\nplanner chooses between approaches.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-c-distributed-ai-work\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example C – Distributed AI\nwork<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Jobs → Queue → Available workers → Results<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The queue organises the order of available tasks. Initially, this is\na data structure issue. Only later does technical distribution across\nmultiple machines come into play: what happens if a worker fails, how do\nwe prevent duplicate processing and where do we store results? We\naddress these operational questions in M4-L1 and in the AI section\nM6-L6.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Implications for costs and operations:<\/strong> Better\nalgorithms can reduce computing time, energy consumption and cloud\ncosts. Additional indices, on the other hand, require storage space,\nmaintenance effort and, in some circumstances, longer write operations.\nGraphs and cache structures require synchronisation and can be more\ndifficult to operate when there are frequent changes. The entire amount\nof work therefore matters when making a choice, not merely the speed of\nan individual search.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide: What is appropriate and when?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Initial situation<\/th>\n<th>Often a sensible starting point<\/th>\n<th>When more is needed<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Few entries, infrequent searches<\/td>\n<td>Simple list/array<\/td>\n<td>When search times become noticeable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exact search by unique ID<\/td>\n<td>Hash table or database index<\/td>\n<td>For range queries or sorting requirements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sorted order and range search<\/td>\n<td>Balanced tree / database index<\/td>\n<td>When storage and write costs become problematic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Process jobs in order<\/td>\n<td>Queue<\/td>\n<td>When failure handling, retries and distribution are needed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dependencies, paths, networks<\/td>\n<td>Graph<\/td>\n<td>When the underlying model becomes too large or dynamic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeated similar subproblems<\/td>\n<td>Dynamic programming<\/td>\n<td>When the cost of intermediate storage outweighs the benefit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Understand first rather than scale first:<\/strong> An\ninefficient algorithm does not automatically become good when it runs on\na larger server. It is often more economical to improve the search\nstrategy, data model or query plan before renting additional\ninfrastructure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Algorithms and data structures form a pair:<\/strong> the\nstored form determines which processing operations are simple or\nexpensive.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Searching and sorting are fundamental building\nblocks:<\/strong> linear search, binary search and hashing meet different\nrequirements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Arrays, lists, stacks, queues, trees and graphs<\/strong>\norganise data for different access patterns.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Recursion, dynamic programming and greedy\nstrategies<\/strong> are general design methods, but not universal\nsolutions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Big-O notation describes growth<\/strong>, not measured\nseconds. The average case, worst case and memory consumption must be\nconsidered separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Fundamental uncomputability and high computational cost are\ndifferent problems.<\/strong> More hardware does not remove every\nlimit.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Next lesson: <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4 –\nSoftware engineering and system design<\/a>.<\/strong> It deals with the\norganisation of <em>entire applications<\/em>: modules, interfaces,\ntests, version control and the question of when a simple application is\npreferable to a system comprising many services.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026; specific clarification of\ncost bounds on 05.11.2026. This lesson deals primarily with enduring\nfundamentals; it contains no current provider prices or short-lived\nproduct claims.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), <em>6.006 Introduction\nto Algorithms<\/em>, lecture notes (fundamentals of asymptotic analysis,\nhashing, sorting, graph search and dynamic programming): <a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2008/pages/lecture-notes/\">https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2008/pages/lecture-notes/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Pat Morin, <em>Open Data Structures: An Introduction<\/em>, Athabasca\nUniversity Press (2013): <a\nhref=\"https://www.aupress.ca/books/120226-open-data-structures/\">https://www.aupress.ca/books/120226-open-data-structures/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SQLite Project, <em>Query Planning<\/em> (search indices, sorting and\nquery plans): <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/queryplanner.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/queryplanner.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The examples, architectural sketches and comparison table are\nteaching simplifications. Actual running times depend on data, hardware,\nimplementation and the storage structure used.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M1-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M1-L4 – Software engineering and system design<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: Foundations of computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5 – Data and databases<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview – When a program becomes a long-lived system<\/h2>\n<p>A small program may consist of a few manageable functions. It reads a\nfile, processes data and displays a result. Later, however, it may need\nto manage users, communicate with a database, receive updates and be\naccessible on the internet. The main difficulty is then no longer simply\nwriting a particular algorithm: <strong>The entire system must remain\nunderstandable, testable and reliable even after many\nchanges.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Software engineering<\/strong> deals with methods and tools\nfor developing software in a planned manner and maintaining it in the\nlong term. <strong>System design<\/strong> (software design or\narchitecture) determines the major building blocks, their\nresponsibilities and how they work together. An architecture need not be\nlarge: even a small PHP project has an architecture, whether or not it\nhas ever been explicitly documented.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"initial-problem-and-mini-architecture\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Initial problem and\nmini-architecture<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User interface\n      │\n      ▼\nApplication logic ──► File or database access\n      │\n      ▼\nFeedback to user<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>As long as these tasks are clearly separated, they can often be\nadapted independently. If all inputs, database accesses, calculations\nand presentation are mixed in one large block of code, however, every\nsmall change creates greater risk. Software architecture therefore\nprimarily addresses a <strong>maintainability problem<\/strong>, not\nnecessarily a performance problem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Modules, interfaces and abstraction<\/h2>\n<h3\nid=\"11-modularitaet-ein-system-in-verantwortlichkeiten-gliedern\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Modularity:\nDividing a system by responsibility<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>module<\/strong> is a cohesive software component with as\nclearly defined a task as possible. A media catalogue might have modules\nfor import, metadata, search and export. They need not be separate\nprograms or servers. A module may simply be a file, a package or a\nclearly delimited part of the same application.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Media catalogue (one application)\n  ├─ Import: recognise and read files\n  ├─ Catalogue: manage entries\n  ├─ Search: identify matches\n  └─ Export: output selected data<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This structure is particularly helpful when making changes: if a new\nimport format is added, ideally the search logic should not need to\nchange. A common design goal is <strong>high cohesion<\/strong> (related\ntasks stay together) with <strong>low coupling<\/strong> (few unnecessary\ndependencies between modules).<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it excessive?<\/strong> A program with ten clear\ninstructions generally does not need an architecture with twelve layers.\nToo many tiny modules can make navigation as difficult as one enormous\nblock.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"12-schnittstellen-verabredungen-zwischen-bausteinen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interfaces:\nAgreements between components<\/h3>\n<p>An <strong>interface<\/strong> describes how a component can be\naccessed and what it returns. It may take the form of a function call\nwithin a program or a network connection between different programs.<\/p>\n<p>For example, the search module receives a search term and returns a\nlist of results. The rest of the application does not need to know\nwhether a simple loop or a database index is used internally.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Interface ──► Search(&quot;winter jacket&quot;)\n                      │\n                Search module\n                      │\n                 Result list\n                      │\nInterface ◄───────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A good interface clearly defines <strong>which inputs are valid, what\nthe result means and which errors can occur<\/strong>. It makes it\npossible to change the internal implementation without modifying every\ncaller. The downside is that a poorly chosen interface published too\nearly can make subsequent changes difficult.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"13-abstraktion-und-kapselung-unterscheiden\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing\nabstraction from encapsulation<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Abstraction<\/strong> omits details that are unimportant for a\nparticular perspective. For the caller, what matters is ‘find a record’,\nnot the search algorithm used.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Encapsulation<\/strong> restricts direct access to a module’s\ninternals. Rather than changing the same internal fields from several\nplaces, a defined operation such as ‘change status’ is used. This is\nparticularly important for shared data, since rules can then be checked\nin one place.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Not ideal: Interface ──► internal tables + internal states\n\nBetter:    Interface ──► defined catalogue functions\n                                      │\n                              hidden implementation<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Encapsulation does not make errors impossible. It does, however,\nreduce the number of places where business rules can be bypassed.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"14-schichtenarchitektur-gleiche-anwendung-verschiedene-aufgaben\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Layered\narchitecture: One application, different tasks<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>layer<\/strong> describes a kind of responsibility. A common\nbut non-mandatory basic model separates presentation, business logic and\ndata access.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Presentation (HTML / desktop interface)\n                │\n                ▼\nBusiness logic (rules, validation, calculation)\n                │\n                ▼\nData access (file, SQLite, external service)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If a local tool is later to be offered as a web application, the\nbusiness logic may be able to remain while the interface is replaced. In\npractice, this is not straightforward for every program: if file paths,\nwindow dialogues and database commands are mixed everywhere,\nrestructuring will require more effort.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> Layers are not a law of nature. Some\napplications benefit from a feature-oriented structure in which all\nparts of a use case are located together. What matters is clear\ndependencies and boundaries.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>APIs and data formats – Programs communicate with one\nanother<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>API<\/strong> (<em>Application Programming Interface<\/em>)\ndefines how software functions can be used. Within an application this\ncan be a function call; a <strong>web API<\/strong>, by contrast, is\naccessible over a network. The ‘A’ in API does not therefore\nautomatically imply internet access.<\/p>\n<p>A possible web API for a media catalogue might answer the request\n‘Return the data for entry 4711’. This often happens via\n<strong>HTTP<\/strong> (<em>Hypertext Transfer Protocol<\/em>, a web\ntransfer protocol), which we discuss more closely in M1-L6.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser / other program\n        │ Request: entry 4711\n        ▼\n      Web API\n        │\n        ▼\n   Catalogue logic\n        │\n        ▼\n      Database\n        │\n        └─► Response with data or error message<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The response requires an agreed <strong>data format<\/strong>.\nExamples are <strong>JSON<\/strong> (<em>JavaScript Object Notation<\/em>,\na structured text format), <strong>XML<\/strong> (<em>Extensible Markup\nLanguage<\/em>) and <strong>CSV<\/strong> (<em>Comma-Separated\nValues<\/em>, tabular text data). JSON is often suitable for nested\nobjects; CSV for simple tables. The appropriate format depends on the\nshape of the data and the participants, not on buzzwords.<\/p>\n<p>An API must govern more than the format: which fields are required?\nIs an identifier a string or a number? What happens when records are\nmissing? Which users may make changes? How do existing clients remain\nfunctional when the API changes? These agreements are called an\n<strong>interface contract<\/strong>, or simply a <em>contract<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> For an isolated desktop\nprogram that reads and writes its own data, a public web API is\ngenerally excessive. A clearly defined internal interface can still be\nuseful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State machines and events – Making behaviour visible<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"31-zustandsautomaten-statt-unuebersichtlicher-sonderfaelle\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State\nmachines instead of confusing special cases<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>state machine<\/strong> describes the possible states of a\nsystem and the permitted transitions. For example, a background job may\nbe ‘new’, ‘in progress’, ‘completed’ or ‘failed’. A completed job must\nnot be arbitrarily reset to ‘new’ simply because a user refreshes a\npage.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>         Job received\n               │\n               ▼\n              NEW\n               │ Worker takes over\n               ▼\n          IN PROGRESS\n           /       \\\n  Success /         \\ Error\n         ▼           ▼\n    COMPLETED      FAILED\n                      │ Retry according to rule\n                      └────────► NEW<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The advantage is that for every transition it is possible to specify\nwho may trigger it and which data are changed. Such models are\nparticularly useful for orders, payments, uploads and AI processing\njobs. The limitation is that a state machine covers only the explicitly\nmodelled cases; real systems may need additional states, such as\n‘cancelled’ or ‘awaiting approval’.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"32-ereignisverarbeitung-etwas-ist-passiert\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Event processing:\n‘Something has happened’<\/h3>\n<p>In <strong>event-driven processing<\/strong> (<em>Event-Driven\nArchitecture<\/em>), a component reacts to an event that has occurred,\nsuch as ‘video uploaded’. One component may process the file, another\ngenerate thumbnails and a third prepare a notification.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Upload completed\n         │\n         ▼\n Event &quot;File arrived&quot;\n         │\n     ┌───┴──────────┐\n     ▼              ▼\nRead metadata   Generate preview\n     │              │\n     └──────┬───────┘\n            ▼\n          Results<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This can happen within <strong>one<\/strong> program, or later via a\nqueue on multiple computers. Event processing and microservices are\ntherefore not the same thing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitations:<\/strong> Events may arrive late, more than once\nor in an unexpected order. A system must then define whether duplicate\nprocessing is permitted. For a very simple action that can be performed\nimmediately, a direct function call is usually clearer.<\/p>\n<p>An important protective concept is <strong>idempotence<\/strong>: if\nthe same operation is executed again because of a repeated message, this\nmust not produce an unintended second business effect. For example, an\nupload with the unique job identifier <code>4711<\/code> is reported\ntwice. The program checks the identifier and does not accidentally\ncreate two chargeable processing jobs. This can be implemented using\nunique keys, controlled state transitions or a separate processing\nhistory. Idempotence does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that every technical\nintermediate step is executed only once; what matters is that the\nbusiness result remains correct when the operation is repeated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Concurrent retries:<\/strong> Simply asking ‘Has job 4711\nalready been completed?’ before writing is not always sufficient: two\nworkers might read ‘no’ at the same time and then both create a new job.\nIf protection relies on a database, a <strong>unique key with an atomic\ninsert attempt<\/strong> followed by a state check can, for example,\nprevent a duplicate <em>local<\/em> change. This does not yet guarantee\nexactly one external payment, email or other effect; additional\nidempotency keys of the target system or an appropriate distributed\nworkflow may be required. For further study, see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Tests – Checking requirements without claiming the absence\nof errors<\/h2>\n<p>Software contains errors even when developed carefully. Tests can\ncompare <strong>specifically observed behaviour<\/strong> with\nrequirements and reduce risks; however, they do not normally prove that\na complex system is free from errors in every conceivable situation. A\npassed test is evidence for the cases tested, not a general proof of\ncorrectness (see <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a>).<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of test<\/th>\n<th>Primarily checks<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Typical limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Unit test<\/strong> (component test)<\/td>\n<td>Small function or unit<\/td>\n<td>Does the filter sort the results correctly?<\/td>\n<td>Often does not detect interaction errors<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Integration test<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Interaction between real components<\/td>\n<td>Does the application store a record and read it back correctly?<\/td>\n<td>Requires more infrastructure and maintenance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>End-to-end test<\/strong> (E2E)<\/td>\n<td>A complete workflow<\/td>\n<td>A user signs in and exports a report<\/td>\n<td>Slower, may be vulnerable to environmental problems<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Contract test<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Agreed API structure<\/td>\n<td>Are the required fields present in a response?<\/td>\n<td>Does not automatically check all business logic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security testing<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Selected vulnerabilities<\/td>\n<td>Is unauthorised access to other users’ data blocked?<\/td>\n<td>No substitute for secure architecture or ongoing maintenance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A <strong>regression test<\/strong> checks whether a change has\ninadvertently broken behaviour that already worked; it may consist of\nseveral of the types of tests mentioned above. A realistic collection of\ntests covers, as far as possible, normal cases, edge cases and error\npaths: empty inputs, invalid identifiers, interrupted connections and\nunexpected data.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-workflow\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-workflow<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code> Change to program\n         │\n         ▼\nSmall tests ──► Integration tests ──► A few important complete workflows\n         │                                │\n         └───────── Error? ───────────────┘\n                      │\n                      ▼\n                   Fix cause<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>When is it excessive?<\/strong> A short-lived personal utility\nscript may not need a fully automated E2E testing infrastructure.\nNevertheless, even a small reproducible test case can prevent the same\nerror recurring. Systematic testing becomes considerably more important\nfor paid services and other users’ data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Version control and deployment – Keeping changes\nmanageable<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"51-git-und-versionen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Git and versions<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Version control<\/strong> records changes to files so that\nthey can be traced. <strong>Git<\/strong> is a widely used distributed\nversion control system. A <strong>commit<\/strong> records a particular\nstate of changes. A <strong>branch<\/strong> allows work on a variant\nwithout immediately changing the main line of development. A\n<strong>release version<\/strong>, by contrast, is a deliberately\npublished state of the software; it need not coincide with every\ncommit.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Stable version ───────────► New release\n       │                        ▲\n       └─► Development branch ──┘\n             Check changes<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This does not automatically prevent bad changes, but it makes them\nvisible and, if necessary, reversible. Git is not a substitute for\nexternal backups and does not store ongoing database transactions.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"52-ci-aenderungen-automatisch-pruefen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>CI: Checking changes\nautomatically<\/h3>\n<p><strong>CI<\/strong> (<em>Continuous Integration<\/em>) describes\nfrequently merging and automatically checking code changes. After a\nchange, a service can, for example, build the software, run tests and\nwarn of notable problems. This is particularly useful when several\ndevelopers are involved, but can also relieve individual developers of\nrecurring checks.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Change in Git\n      │\n      ▼\nAutomated build\n      │\n      ▼\nTests / checks\n      │\n      ├─ Failure ──► Revise change\n      │\n      └─ Success ─► Release possible<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>CD<\/strong> is ambiguous and should be specified more\nprecisely:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Continuous Delivery<\/strong> means that a checked version\ncan be released with little additional effort; the actual release may\ndeliberately remain manual.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Continuous Deployment<\/strong> means that changes which pass\nthe specified checks are automatically deployed to production.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>An automated pipeline is a chain of such steps, not necessarily a\nlarge cloud system. The official GitHub Actions documentation describes\nCI checks, automated deployments and approval gates. It is a\n<strong>product example<\/strong>, not the definition of the general\nprinciples.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational risks:<\/strong> A green test indicator means only\nthat the defined checks were passed. Faulty checks, compromised\ndependencies or inadequate release permissions remain possible.\nUncontrolled automatic production deployment can distribute an error to\nall users very quickly. For a small local tool, manually tested,\nversioned releases are often sufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Monoliths and microservices – An architectural decision, not\na ranking of progress<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>monolith<\/strong> is an application built and deployed as a\nsingle unit. This initially says nothing about how well it is organised\ninternally. A well-structured monolith may consist of many clearly\nseparated modules.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Microservices<\/strong>, by contrast, are multiple smaller\nservices separated according to business responsibilities, which can be\ndeployed independently and usually work together via network\ninterfaces.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"two-variants-of-the-same-business-task\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two variants of the same\nbusiness task<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>VARIANT A – MODULAR MONOLITH\n\nBrowser ──► [Web application: User | Catalogue | Search]\n                                  │\n                               Database\n\nVARIANT B – MICROSERVICES\n\nBrowser ──► [API entry point]\n                │\n          ┌─────┼────────┐\n          ▼     ▼        ▼\n        User Catalogue Search\n          │     │        │\n        separate responsibilities / data access<\/code><\/pre>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Modular monolith<\/th>\n<th>Microservices<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Changes to several business areas<\/td>\n<td>Often possible in a single release<\/td>\n<td>Independent releases possible; coordination may be necessary<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network communication between modules<\/td>\n<td>Often local function calls<\/td>\n<td>Additional network failures and latency<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operations<\/td>\n<td>Fewer separate services<\/td>\n<td>More deployments, access permissions and monitoring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaling<\/td>\n<td>Often the entire application initially<\/td>\n<td>Individual services can be scaled as appropriate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data consistency<\/td>\n<td>Often easier within one database transaction<\/td>\n<td>More difficult across service boundaries<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small individual developer projects<\/td>\n<td>Often very appropriate<\/td>\n<td>Frequently unnecessary effort<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>When do microservices help?<\/strong> For example, when\ndifferent teams need to work independently or one part consistently has\nmarkedly different performance and availability requirements.\n<strong>When are they less suitable?<\/strong> When an individual\ndeveloper offers a manageable online tool with a limited number of\nusers. Here, a simple, cleanly modularised monolith often provides\ngreater benefit.<\/p>\n<p>The architecture author Martin Fowler describes both benefits such as\nindependent deployment and costs arising from distributed data storage\nand more complex operations. This implies <strong>no universal\nrule<\/strong>, but an important warning: many small services are not\nautomatically better than one carefully developed program.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Maintainability, technical debt and documentation<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Maintainability<\/strong> describes how easily a system can be\nunderstood, corrected, tested and extended. This includes meaningful\nnames, manageable modules, clear error handling, a clear data structure\nand brief documentation of important decisions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Technical debt<\/strong> consists of deliberate or\nunintentional shortcuts that may make future changes more expensive. For\nexample, owing to time constraints, every part of a program accesses\ndatabase tables directly. This works initially but makes a later change\nto the data structure expensive. Not every simple solution is technical\ndebt: simplicity may be precisely what makes an architecture more\ndurable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Quick workaround\n       │\n       ▼\nLess development effort in the short term\n       │\n       └─► If repeated frequently:\n             Changes become riskier\n             Testing becomes more demanding\n             Restructuring becomes more expensive<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Useful documentation need not be a large collection of texts. An\narchitectural overview, the major interfaces, data formats, known\nlimitations and the reasons for unusual decisions are often enough.\nDocumentation itself has maintenance costs: outdated instructions can be\nmore harmful than brief, up-to-date notes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security begins with design<\/h2>\n<p>Security is not a problem only when software becomes publicly\naccessible on the internet. System design should already establish which\ndata need protection and who may perform which actions.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Authentication<\/strong> establishes who is signing in.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Authorisation<\/strong> establishes which actions that user\nmay perform. Successful authentication does not automatically grant\naccess to every record.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Least privilege<\/strong> means that modules and users\nreceive only the permissions they need.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Input validation<\/strong> checks whether received data have\nthe expected types, sizes and values; it does not replace appropriate\nsecurity measures such as parameterised database queries.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Secrets<\/strong> such as access tokens and passwords must\nnot be hard-coded in published source code or included in logs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Secure defaults<\/strong> ensure that unnecessary features\nand permissions do not have to be disabled retrospectively.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-no-direct-access-to-other-users-data\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nNo direct access to other users’ data<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Authenticated user\n        │ Request for record 4711\n        ▼\n   Web application\n        │\n        ▼\nCheck: May this user read 4711?\n       / \\\n     yes  no\n     │      │\n     ▼      ▼\n Response  Access denied<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>These principles are also stated in the OWASP guidelines (<em>Open\nWorldwide Application Security Project<\/em>, an international security\nproject). For a purely local data converter some measures are simpler,\nwhereas for a public multi-user system they are indispensable. A\nsecurity concept must also take into account updates, backups and\nincident response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical scenarios – From a desktop tool to an\nonline service<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A: Local media\ncatalogue<\/h3>\n<p>You have a tool that recognises video files, imports metadata and\nexports a report. A sensible architecture could comprise an import\nmodule, catalogue logic, search and export. Everything runs in a single\nprocess and can use a local file or SQLite database. Neither a web API\nnor containers nor microservices are needed.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-the-same-tool-as-a-paid-online-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B: The\nsame tool as a paid online service<\/h3>\n<p>Later, other users access it via a browser. The system now needs a\nweb interface, protected sign-in, access rules, reliable data storage\nand a process for deploying new versions. This is often still possible\nas <strong>one<\/strong> application with a database.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ──► Web application\n                 │\n           ┌─────┴───────┐\n           ▼             ▼\n      User permissions  Catalogue logic\n                         │\n                      Database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Operating costs arise: hosting, backups, security updates, possibly\nemail delivery, support and legal obligations. The architecture should\nenable such tasks, not make them more difficult through unnecessary\ntechnical diversity.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-c-background-ai-jobs\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example C: Background AI jobs<\/h3>\n<p>An application processes uploaded images with a local or remote AI\nmodel. Such computations sometimes take longer than an ordinary web\nrequest. A <strong>background worker<\/strong> can then be useful,\nprocessing jobs independently of the web interface.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ──► Web application ──► Job list\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n                             AI worker\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n                             Result file<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Multiple servers are not immediately required for this: initially,\nthe web application and worker can run on the same computer. Only higher\nloads or differing hardware requirements make separation worthwhile.\nDetails on queues, retries and distributed operations follow in M4-L1\nand M6-L6.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_004_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Asynchronous background job: The browser sends a request to a web API, which places the job in a queue; a worker takes the job and stores the status or result.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Asynchronous background job: The browser\nsends a request to a web API, which places the job in a queue; a worker\ntakes the job and stores the status or result.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schematic workflow of a longer job: for example, the API\ncan return <strong>202 Accepted<\/strong> and a job identifier before\nexecution is complete. The status is queried later. Whether the queue\nprovides persistent storage and how it redelivers jobs after failures\ndepend on the specific implementation; idempotence for repeated effects\nmust be ensured separately (see M4-L1).<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide – How much architecture is appropriate?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Often a good starting point<\/th>\n<th>Reason for expansion<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A small utility program<\/td>\n<td>A few clear functions<\/td>\n<td>Recurring extensions and shared rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium-sized desktop catalogue<\/td>\n<td>Modules with defined interfaces<\/td>\n<td>Very different access methods or data sources<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A small web application<\/td>\n<td>Modular monolith<\/td>\n<td>Separate teams, substantial differences in load or particular\navailability requirements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public API<\/td>\n<td>Clear contract, permission checks, tests<\/td>\n<td>Many independent integrations and versions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time-consuming AI processing<\/td>\n<td>Background job and worker<\/td>\n<td>Multiple computers or GPU types, queues, fault tolerance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Guiding question:<\/strong> What specific problem does the\nadditional layer, service or automation solve? If no credible answer can\nbe found, the more complex technology is probably not needed yet.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Modularity, abstraction and encapsulation<\/strong> help\nmanage the internal complexity of an application.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Interfaces and data formats<\/strong> make collaboration\nbetween components verifiable; an API is not necessarily an internet\nservice.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>State machines and events<\/strong> describe when particular\nprocessing is permitted or triggered.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Tests, version control and CI/CD<\/strong> help check and\ndeploy changes in a traceable way; they do not guarantee the absence of\nerrors.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Monoliths and microservices<\/strong> are different\norganisational solutions. For small applications a modular monolith can\nbe simpler and more economical in the long term.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Maintainability and security<\/strong> belong in the design\nfrom the outset, not just when fixing errors after launch.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Next lesson: <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5 –\nData and databases<\/a>.<\/strong> There we consider how data can be\nstored reliably, linked together, indexed and changed in transactions –\na foundation for later cluster and cloud databases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026; additions on idempotence\nand the interpretation of tests on 05.11.2026. General architectural\nprinciples are stable over time; product details concerning GitHub\nActions and security guidelines were checked against the official\ndocumentation current at that time. Examples and diagrams are simplified\nfor teaching purposes.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">GitHub Docs, <em>Continuous integration<\/em> (CI, builds, tests): <a\nhref=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/continuous-integration\">https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/continuous-integration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">GitHub Docs, <em>Continuous deployment<\/em> (automated deployment,\napprovals): <a\nhref=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/continuous-deployment\">https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/continuous-deployment<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">GitHub Docs, <em>Understanding GitHub Actions<\/em> (workflow,\nbuild/test/deployment pipeline): <a\nhref=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/understand-github-actions\">https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/understand-github-actions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Martin Fowler, <em>Microservice Trade-Offs<\/em> (benefits and\noperating costs of distributed services, 2015): <a\nhref=\"https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservice-trade-offs.html\">https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservice-trade-offs.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Martin Fowler, <em>Monolith First<\/em> (caution when splitting small\napplications early, 2015): <a\nhref=\"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/MonolithFirst.html\">https://martinfowler.com/bliki/MonolithFirst.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">OWASP Cheat Sheet Series, <em>Secure Product Design<\/em> (security\nin design): <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secure_Product_Design_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secure_Product_Design_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">OWASP Cheat Sheet Series, <em>Authorization<\/em> (permission\nchecking and least privilege): <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M1-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M1-L5 – Data and databases<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: Foundations of computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Why data management is more than saving a\nfile<\/h2>\n<p>An application often needs not only to <em>calculate<\/em> something\nbut also to <em>remember<\/em> something: users’ names, the properties of\na video, the contents of an order, or the processing status of an AI\njob. This information should remain available after the program has\nended. It must also be retrievable, modifiable and — where there are\nmultiple users — as consistent as possible.<\/p>\n<p>All information could be stored in text files. This works for small,\nmanageable programs. It becomes more difficult when many entries are\nrelated, are changed simultaneously, or when a request such as “Show the\ntwenty most recent videos in this category, but only those not yet\nprocessed” must be answered quickly. <strong>Database systems do not\nsimply solve the problem of storage; they bring together methods for\nstructured access, integrity and controlled changes.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This lesson covers files, relational databases, data models, keys,\nthe SQL query language, indexes, transactions and alternatives such as\ndocument and key–value stores. Distribution across multiple servers is\n<em>not<\/em> yet the subject: that is covered in detail in lessons\nM3-L1–M3-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Files, databases and database management systems<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>file<\/strong> is first of all a unit that a file system\nmanages persistently. CSV (<em>Comma-Separated Values<\/em>, a simple\ntext-based tabular format), JSON (<em>JavaScript Object Notation<\/em>, a\nstructured text format) and Markdown are possible file contents. They\nare often ideal for exporting, exchanging, configuring or archiving\ndocuments.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>database<\/strong> is an organised collection of related\ndata. The <strong>database management system (DBMS)<\/strong> is the\nsoftware that manages these data: it receives queries, reads and writes\ndata, checks certain rules and organises access. In everyday language\nboth are often referred to together as a “database”.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application\n    │  &quot;Find media with tag X&quot;\n    ▼\nDatabase management system (DBMS)\n    │  Search, permissions, integrity, writes\n    ▼\nPersistent data (file(s), storage structures, indexes)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> The distinction between a file and a\ndatabase is not a physical either-or question. For example, an SQLite\ndatabase is often itself a file on the storage medium. The difference\nlies in the rules and functions used to manage that file.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"when-is-a-file-sufficient\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When is a file sufficient?<\/h3>\n<p>Markdown files are particularly suitable for our course: each lesson\nis a self-contained text, easily backed up and readable without special\nsoftware. An SQLite database for all the teaching texts would offer\nadditional benefits only if, for example, we wanted to manage complex\nlinks, a search index or individual reading progress. It need not\ntherefore become the authoritative content source.<\/p>\n<p>A small local program with a rarely changed list can also use a JSON\nor CSV file. A database becomes valuable when multiple properties,\nrelationships, queries and simultaneous changes must be systematically\ncoordinated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The relational model: tables, rows and columns<\/h2>\n<p>In a <strong>relational database system<\/strong>, data are logically\norganised as relations — usually called tables in everyday language. A\ntable contains rows (records) and columns (properties). The tables\nfollow a defined schema.<\/p>\n<p>Example of a media catalogue:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Table <code>Medien<\/code><\/strong><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">medien_id<\/th>\n<th>titel<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">jahr<\/th>\n<th>status<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">101<\/td>\n<td>Wintervideo<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2025<\/td>\n<td>geprüft<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">102<\/td>\n<td>Stadtaufnahme<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2026<\/td>\n<td>offen<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">103<\/td>\n<td>Kurzfilm<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2024<\/td>\n<td>geprüft<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The <code>medien_id<\/code> identifies the record. Two media items may\nhave the same title; the ID should nevertheless be unique.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A schema<\/strong> specifies which tables, columns, data types\nand rules are provided. For example, a year can be defined as an\ninteger, the title as text and an ID as a unique key. A schema does not\nreplace every domain-specific check: a numeric field, for example, does\nnot automatically prevent an implausible year value unless an\nappropriate rule is in place.<\/p>\n<p>The decisive advantage is that the DBMS can answer structured\nquestions without the application having to inspect every file and\nrecord manually.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Keys, relationships and referential integrity<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>primary key (Primary Key)<\/strong> uniquely identifies a\nrow within a table. A <strong>foreign key (Foreign Key)<\/strong> refers\nto a corresponding key in another table. Foreign keys can enforce\nrelationships: an order, for instance, may refer only to an existing\ncustomer.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose a media catalogue is also to contain channels:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Channels (Kanäle)\n  kanal_id = 7, name = &quot;Technik&quot;\n  kanal_id = 8, name = &quot;Natur&quot;\n          │\n          │ One channel can contain several media items\n          ▼\nMedia (Medien)\n  medien_id = 101, kanal_id = 7\n  medien_id = 102, kanal_id = 7\n  medien_id = 103, kanal_id = 8<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><code>kanal_id<\/code> is the primary key in the <code>Kanäle<\/code>\ntable and a foreign key in the <code>Medien<\/code> table. Consequently,\nthe channel name need not be stored again in every media row.<\/p>\n<p>There are different relationship types:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>One-to-one (1:1):<\/strong> A user has exactly one associated\nprofile entry — <em>if that is how the domain is defined<\/em>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>One-to-many (1:n):<\/strong> A channel contains many media\nitems; in this simplified model, a media item belongs to one\nchannel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Many-to-many (n:m):<\/strong> A media item can have several\ntags, and a tag can occur on many media items. This relationship is\nnormally represented using a separate association table,\n<code>Medien_Tags<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Referential integrity<\/strong> prevents references to\nnon-existent records, provided that the corresponding rules are enabled\nand modelled correctly. The consequences of deleting a linked entry must\nbe explicitly decided: prohibiting deletion, removing references or also\ndeleting dependent records are different options with different\nrisks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>SQLite-specific detail:<\/strong> SQLite supports foreign\nkeys, but enforcement is not automatically enabled in typical\nconfigurations. Applications should explicitly set and check the desired\nsetting for their connections. PostgreSQL uses different defaults here.\nSuch details are a good illustration of why <em>the relational\nmodel<\/em> and <em>a particular database product<\/em> are not the same\nthing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>SQL: The language is not the database system<\/h2>\n<p><strong>SQL<\/strong> stands for <em>Structured Query Language<\/em>.\nSQL is a language used particularly to define, query and modify\nrelational data. <strong>SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL and Oracle Database,\nby contrast, are particular database systems<\/strong> that support SQL —\nwith different extensions, type rules, features and operating\nmodels.<\/p>\n<p>The product name <strong>Oracle Database<\/strong> refers to a\nspecific database system; <strong>Oracle<\/strong> alone may also refer\nto the company or other Oracle products, depending on the context.<\/p>\n<p>SQL is primarily <strong>declarative<\/strong>: one describes\n<em>which<\/em> records are wanted, rather than the exact sequence of\naccess steps. The DBMS chooses an execution plan for this purpose.<\/p>\n<p>Three short examples to read (not an exercise):<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> titel, jahr <span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> Medien <span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> status <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;geprüft&#39;<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This means: return the title and year of records whose status is\n“geprüft” (checked).<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb4\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb4-1\"><a href=\"#cb4-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> Medien (medien_id, titel, jahr, status)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb4-2\"><a href=\"#cb4-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">104<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Archivfilm&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2023<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;offen&#39;<\/span>);<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This inserts a new record. A real schema may require additional\ncolumns and rules; the examples serve only to help readers understand\nthe language.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb5\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb5-1\"><a href=\"#cb5-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">UPDATE<\/span> Medien <span class=\"kw\">SET<\/span> status <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;geprüft&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> medien_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">104<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This changes the status of the selected media item. The\n<code>WHERE<\/code> condition is especially important: without an\nappropriate restriction, an update statement might affect very many\nrows.<\/p>\n<p>Overview of important SQL keywords: <code>SELECT<\/code> (query),\n<code>INSERT<\/code> (insert), <code>UPDATE<\/code> (modify),\n<code>DELETE<\/code> (delete), <code>WHERE<\/code> (condition),\n<code>JOIN<\/code> (combine tables), <code>ORDER BY<\/code> (sort),\n<code>GROUP BY<\/code> (group). Here too, the exact range of features\ndepends on the particular system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Queries across relationships: the JOIN<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_005_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Schema with customer, order and product tables: primary and foreign keys connect tables for a JOIN.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schema with customer, order and product\ntables: primary and foreign keys connect tables for a JOIN.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustration: A simplified example combining customers, orders\nand products. The result rows are illustrative sample data.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>One of the great strengths of relational databases is the targeted\ncombination of information from multiple tables. A <strong>JOIN<\/strong>\ncombines rows according to a specified relationship.<\/p>\n<p>Example: <code>Medien<\/code> contains <code>kanal_id<\/code>; the name\nappears only in <code>Kanäle<\/code>. The question “Which channel does\nthis media item belong to?” can connect the two tables.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Table Medien                      Table Kanäle\nmedien_id | kanal_id              kanal_id | name\n101       | 7                 ───► 7        | Technik\n102       | 7                 ───► 7        | Technik\n103       | 8                 ───► 8        | Natur\n                   │\n                   ▼\nResult: media item 101 – Technik, media item 102 – Technik, ...<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The DBMS can thus return a combined answer without storing the\nchannel name again in each media record. Different types of JOIN\ndetermine whether only matching rows or also entries without a\ncounterpart are included.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> With very large amounts of data or\npoorly chosen queries, JOINs can be expensive. Across multiple\n<em>separate database servers<\/em>, they are particularly challenging —\na topic of the later lesson on sharding, M3-L3.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data modelling and normalisation<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Data modelling<\/strong> means specifying which objects an\napplication knows about, what properties they have and how they are\nconnected. This already determines whether a database will remain\ncomprehensible and reliable later.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_006_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Example of database normalisation: a table with repeated customer details is split into separate customer, product, order and line-item tables with primary and foreign keys.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Repeated customer details in an order table are\nseparated into customers, products, orders and order-item tables linked\nby keys.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Avoid repeated customer data: separate tables and key relationships\nfacilitate consistent changes.<\/p>\n<p>A poor media model might store all tags as text in one field:\n<code>technik, video, berlin<\/code>. This initially seems simple. It\nbecomes more difficult to search reliably for individual tags,\nstandardise spellings or rename a tag.<\/p>\n<p>A more structured model separates <code>Medien<\/code>,\n<code>Tags<\/code> and <code>Medien_Tags<\/code>. Each tag is then\nrecorded once and can be assigned to multiple media items through\nassociations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Normalisation<\/strong> is the systematic organisation of\nrelational data in order to avoid unnecessary repetition and certain\nkinds of update error. Consider these examples:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Insertion anomaly:<\/strong> A new channel might require a\nfictitious media record if the channel and the media item are\ninseparable in one table.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Update anomaly:<\/strong> A channel name appears one hundred\ntimes in the table and is changed in only 99 rows.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Deletion anomaly:<\/strong> Deleting the last media item\naccidentally removes the only information about the channel as\nwell.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The <strong>normal forms<\/strong> are a widely used guide for\nmodelling. In the classical relational interpretation, first normal form\nrequires clearly delimited, atomic values according to the data model.\nHigher normal forms address dependencies on keys and among attributes. A\nformal theory of proofs is not initially necessary to understand this;\nthe underlying problem of inconsistent information stored multiple times\nis what matters.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When can less normalisation make sense?<\/strong> For\nfrequently required read views or reports, derived values or summaries\nare sometimes deliberately maintained. This\n<strong>denormalisation<\/strong> can speed up queries, but creates\nadditional synchronisation obligations. Not all duplication is an error;\nuncontrolled duplication is risky.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Indexes: Why millions of records remain searchable<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>index<\/strong> is an additional data structure that speeds\nup access to particular columns or values. It can be compared to the\nsubject index at the back of a textbook: to find pages on a particular\nterm, one need not read the entire book from the beginning.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose the media table contains several million rows. Without a\nsuitable index, a system must examine very many rows for some queries.\nAn index on <code>medien_id<\/code>, <code>kanal_id<\/code> or frequently\nsearched properties can help it find matching entries considerably\nfaster.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Without a suitable index:             With a suitable index:\nQuery                                 Query\n   │                                     │\n   ▼                                     ▼\nExamine many table rows              Search index for value\n   │                                     │\n   ▼                                     ▼\nMatches                               Reference to matching rows<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Many database systems use variants of <strong>B-trees<\/strong> for\nordinary indexes, supporting ordered searches. This is not the same as\nsimple binary search in an array, but it follows a similar principle:\nchecking each entry individually is avoided. Hash-based structures are\nalso possible for certain systems and queries.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>query planner<\/strong> or optimiser estimates an\nefficient way to execute a query. It can use indexes, but need not\nchoose them for every query. For small numbers of rows or very many\nmatches, a table scan may be cheaper.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"the-cost-of-an-index\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The cost of an index<\/h3>\n<p>Indexes consume additional storage space and must be maintained when\naffected records are inserted, changed or deleted. Many indexes can slow\ndown write operations. Furthermore, not every index helps every query:\nthe order of columns, filters, data distribution and sorting influence\nits use.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Practical consequence:<\/strong> An index is a targeted\nresponse to recurring access requirements, not a universally free speed\nboost. A query plan can help determine which query is actually slow. The\ndetails follow in M3-L1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transactions: Treating related changes as a unit<\/h2>\n<p>A database system must not only find the correct row but also handle\nrelated changes reliably. This is the purpose of\n<strong>transactions<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Consider a booking: a customer receives a paid service. The system\nshould record both a payment entry and the activation of the customer’s\naccess. If only one of these steps is stored, an inconsistent state can\narise.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                   Transaction\n                        │\n            ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n            ▼                       ▼\n       Record booking          Activate access\n            │                       │\n            └───────────┬───────────┘\n                        ▼\n              All steps successful?\n                  /          \\\n                yes           no\n                 │             │\n                 ▼             ▼\n           COMMIT: keep  ROLLBACK: undo<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>For a course booking, the abbreviation <strong>ACID<\/strong> can be\nunderstood through four questions. A seat reservation and its associated\nreservation record are stored in the same database.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What happens if saving fails halfway through?\nAtomicity.<\/strong> The reserved seat and the reservation record belong\nto the same booking. If recording fails, the seat reservation is also\nundone.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Which states may the booking leave behind?\nConsistency.<\/strong> A valid seat must exist for every reservation\nrecord. This rule can be enforced with a foreign key. The application\nmust implement other domain-specific rules so that every successful\nbooking complies with them.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What happens when users compete for the final seat?\nIsolation.<\/strong> Two orders arriving simultaneously should not both\nreceive the last seat. The isolation level determines which intermediate\nstates each booking may see. Unique allocation also requires a suitable\nrule and an appropriate booking procedure.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What remains after a restart? Durability.<\/strong> After a\nconfirmed commit, the booking must survive a failure insofar as the\nsystem’s promised durability guarantee covers it.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Further reading: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/289.291\">Theo\nHärder and Andreas Reuter, Principles of Transaction-Oriented Database\nRecovery (1983), section 1.1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>. The <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-transactions.html\">PostgreSQL\ndocumentation on transactions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> demonstrates the use of\n<code>COMMIT<\/code> and <code>ROLLBACK<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation of a local database transaction:<\/strong> In the\nteaching example above, the <strong>payment booking as a database\nentry<\/strong> and the <strong>internal activation<\/strong> are two\nchanges to the same database. A local transaction may be suitable for\nthis. An actual charge at an external payment provider, a sent email or\na change in a second independent database, however, is\n<strong>not<\/strong> undone simply because the local database executes\n<code>ROLLBACK<\/code>. Such cross-service processes require their own\nfailure models, idempotency and, where appropriate, patterns such as a\n<strong>transactional outbox<\/strong> (storing the local change and the\nintention to send together) or a <strong>saga<\/strong> (a sequence of\nlocal steps with defined compensating actions). An outbox provides a\nmore reliably traceable notification path, not automatically\nexactly-once delivery to the recipient. These topics are discussed in\ndetail in <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>An important conceptual pitfall:<\/strong> The “C” in ACID\ndoes not mean that data are always automatically <em>factually\ntrue<\/em>. If an application writes an incorrect price and no\nappropriate rule exists, the transaction does not make it correct.\nConsistency refers to defined rules and database invariants.<\/p>\n<p>Likewise, “durability” does not mean that data are protected against\nevery disaster, deliberate deletion or the loss of all storage media.\nBackups and recovery procedures remain necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Many users simultaneously: isolation and conflicts<\/h2>\n<p>In multi-user operation, two processes may read or modify the same\nrecord at the same time. A typical error is the <strong>lost\nupdate<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Stock: 10 items\n\nUser A reads 10             User B reads 10\nA plans: 10 - 2 = 8         B plans: 10 - 3 = 7\nA saves 8                   B saves 7\n\nResult: 7 – but 5 items were actually removed.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A database can provide various mechanisms for handling such conflicts\nin a controlled manner: locks, version checks or suitable isolation\nlevels. However, the particular application must also be designed\ncorrectly. Two logically separate read and write operations do not\nautomatically constitute a safe stock change.<\/p>\n<p>Some database systems use <strong>MVCC<\/strong> (<em>Multi-Version\nConcurrency Control<\/em>). Readers and writers can access different\nversions of a record, so readers need not always wait for every writer.\nPostgreSQL is a well-known example. The view a transaction receives and\nthe conflicts that can occur, however, depend on the isolation level and\nthe particular implementation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Avoid overengineering:<\/strong> A single desktop tool whose\nwrites are controlled by only one process rarely needs the same\nconcurrency mechanisms as an online booking system with thousands of\nsimultaneous users. Transactions remain useful locally; complex\ndistribution and complicated conflict resolution do not always do\nso.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What types of databases are there?<\/h2>\n<p>A relational database is not the only possible form of data\nmanagement. The umbrella term <strong>NoSQL<\/strong> is often\ninterpreted as <em>Not Only SQL<\/em> and encompasses various models that\ndo not primarily rely on classical relational tables and SQL. It is not\na uniform technical architecture and does not automatically mean\n“without SQL”, “faster” or “arbitrarily scalable”.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model<\/th>\n<th>Basic idea<\/th>\n<th>Often suitable tasks<\/th>\n<th>Typical disadvantages<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational<\/td>\n<td>Tables with relationships, structured\nqueries and transactions<\/td>\n<td>Customers, orders, catalogues,\nbookings<\/td>\n<td>Schema and relationships require design;\nlarge-scale distribution may be difficult<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Document-oriented<\/td>\n<td>Entries as related documents with fields\nand substructures<\/td>\n<td>Flexible content objects, varying\nmetadata<\/td>\n<td>Shared data may be duplicated; plan\nrelationships and updates carefully<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key–value<\/td>\n<td>A key points directly to a value<\/td>\n<td>Cache, session data, simple status and\nmapping values<\/td>\n<td>Complex queries often limited or possible\nonly with additional structures<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Graph-oriented<\/td>\n<td>Relationships as edges between nodes are\ncentral<\/td>\n<td>Highly connected recommendations,\ndependency analysis<\/td>\n<td>Additional operation and a different query\nmodel; not necessary for every list<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time-series<\/td>\n<td>Data points are organised by time<\/td>\n<td>Sensor data, measurements, monitoring<\/td>\n<td>Specialised model, often a supplementary\nsystem rather than a universal replacement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These categories are not exclusive. Modern database systems often\nsupport multiple data types and access methods. For example, a\nrelational database can also store JSON data; conversely,\ndocument-oriented systems can provide complex queries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Guiding question:<\/strong> What data relationships and\ncorrectness requirements actually exist? Not: Which database sounds\nparticularly modern?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL and Oracle\ncorrectly<\/h2>\n<p>The most important differences concern not only the query format but\nalso the <strong>operating model<\/strong> and the available\nfeatures:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System<\/th>\n<th>Typical characteristics<\/th>\n<th>Classification<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQLite<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>An SQL database embedded in an\napplication; often a local database file<\/td>\n<td>Very suitable for local tools, mobile\napplications, smaller services and self-contained archives; particular\nlimits for concurrent writes and spatially separated components<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Standalone relational database server with\nextensive SQL, integrity and extension features<\/td>\n<td>Particularly suitable for multi-user\napplications and server-side systems; server operation or a managed\nservice required<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MySQL<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Widely used relational database\nserver<\/td>\n<td>Common in web applications; properties\ndepend on the version, table engine and configuration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oracle Database<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Comprehensive commercial relational\ndatabase system<\/td>\n<td>Common in demanding enterprise\nenvironments; licensing, operating and product decisions can be\ncomplex<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>SQLite is not automatically a “toy database”.<\/strong> It\nsupports SQL, indexes and transactions, and can be very reliable in its\nappropriate area of application. A single server can indeed run a web\napplication with a local SQLite database. If many separate computers are\nto work directly on the same data store at the same time, a\nclient–server system is often more appropriate.<\/p>\n<p><strong>PostgreSQL is not automatically a cluster either.<\/strong> A\nsingle PostgreSQL instance initially runs on one server. Replication,\nhigh availability and sharding are additional concepts and are addressed\nseparately in module M3. The same applies to Oracle Database: the basic\nproduct must be distinguished from vendor-specific clustering and\nsharding features.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What happens when the database reads and writes?<\/h2>\n<p>The exact internals differ between database systems. A typical\nsequence can be imagined as follows:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                    Web application\n                         │\n                     SQL query\n                         ▼\n              Database server / DBMS\n                         │\n                Check / optimise\n                         │\n                  Execution plan\n                         │\n             ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n             ▼                       ▼\n          Cached data           Index / storage\n             └───────────┬───────────┘\n                         ▼\n                     Result set\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                    Web application<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>When writing, depending on the schema and configuration, the system\nchecks data types, keys and other rules, among other things. Transaction\nand logging mechanisms help store confirmed changes in a traceable and\nfailure-resistant manner. Some systems first write to a\n<strong>write-ahead log (WAL)<\/strong>, a log recorded in advance from\nwhich changes can be recovered after a crash. It does\n<strong>not<\/strong> follow that a WAL is a substitute for backups.<\/p>\n<p>More RAM can speed up frequent read accesses. The number of\nconcurrent writes, storage performance and expensive queries can, on the\nother hand, impose limits. In M3-L1–M3-L5 we learn why “more servers”\ndoes not automatically mean “more writable copies of the same data”.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security, backups and ongoing responsibility<\/h2>\n<p>Even the best database structure is not a complete operating concept.\nFor data that are important or personal, at least these questions\nremain:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Access:<\/strong> Which application and which person may view\nand modify which data? The web application’s permission checks must\nremain effective right down to data access.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Input:<\/strong> Parameterised queries prevent user input\nfrom being interpreted as SQL program text. A database alone does not\neliminate insecure application logic.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Loss:<\/strong> What happens in the event of operator error,\ndamaged storage media, malware or a faulty update? A backup must be\nindependently and verifiably recoverable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Changes:<\/strong> How are schema changes and migrations\nchecked before a new software version uses existing data?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Privacy:<\/strong> Which data are actually needed, how long\nare they stored, and how are they deleted or exported when necessary?\nSpecific legal requirements depend on the use case.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Costs:<\/strong> Storage, database servers, backups,\nmonitoring, maintenance and potentially a managed database service\nrequire resources.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Especially important: <strong>A database replica is not a\nbackup.<\/strong> If a record is accidentally deleted, the deletion can\npropagate to other copies. In M3-L2 and M3-L6 we discuss these\ndifferences in detail.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical scenarios<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-a-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario A – Local media\ncatalogue<\/h3>\n<p>A local PureBasic or PHP tool records files, titles, tags,\npublication dates and processing statuses. A single SQLite database is\noften a good starting point. The tables can store media, tags and\nrelationships separately. The user does not need a database server on\nthe internet. Regular, consistent backups of the database file remain\nnecessary.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-b-small-online-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario B – Small online\nservice<\/h3>\n<p>A customer signs in and manages records through a browser. The web\napplication checks permissions; the database provides query, integrity\nand transaction functions.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → web application → permission check → database\n                                                  │\n                                           Tables and indexes<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Such a service can initially be operated as one application plus one\ndatabase. Whether SQLite is sufficient or PostgreSQL is more suitable\ndepends, among other things, on write behaviour, operation and backup\nstrategy. <strong>Multi-user capability alone does not yet require a\ndatabase cluster.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-c-ai-job-management\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario C – AI job\nmanagement<\/h3>\n<p>An online service receives images, creates jobs in a database and has\nthem processed by a separate worker. The database keeps the status, user\nassociation and references to result files; large image or video files\ncan be stored alongside it in a suitable file store.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User → web application → job database\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                          AI worker\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                          Result file\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                       Status in database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If the worker is interrupted, the application must define whether a\njob is restarted and how duplicate processing is avoided. This already\nforms a bridge to queues, idempotent operations and distributed systems\nin M4-L1 and M3-L5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide: When is what unnecessary?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Starting situation<\/th>\n<th>Sensible starting point<\/th>\n<th>Often unnecessary<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reading course with versioned\nchapters<\/td>\n<td>Markdown as source, optionally a search\nindex later<\/td>\n<td>Server database for every section of\ntext<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small personal tool<\/td>\n<td>File or SQLite<\/td>\n<td>Network cluster, sharding<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Web application with manageable usage<\/td>\n<td>One application service with one\ndatabase<\/td>\n<td>Several independent database nodes from\nthe outset<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closely related domain-specific\nbookings<\/td>\n<td>Relational database and well-designed\ntransactions<\/td>\n<td>Replacement with unstructured files\nwithout integrity rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large streams of measurements<\/td>\n<td>Suitable schema; possibly a specialised\ntime-series store<\/td>\n<td>Automatically adopting any “NoSQL” system\nwithout analysing needs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>There is no product-independent rule of the form “from 1,000 users\nonwards, database X must be chosen”. What matters is query patterns,\nconcurrent writes, required correctness, operation, expertise and\ncost.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Files are often sufficient. A DBMS is worthwhile when structured\nqueries, relationships, concurrent changes and integrity rules become\nimportant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Tables, primary keys and foreign keys<\/strong> make\nrelationships verifiable. A well-designed data model prevents many later\nerrors.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>SQL is a language, not a database system.<\/strong> SQLite,\nPostgreSQL, MySQL and Oracle Database implement it in different\nways.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Indexes<\/strong> speed up suitable reads, but require\nstorage and additional work when writing.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Transactions and ACID<\/strong> describe properties of\nrelated changes; they guarantee neither correct business logic nor\nprotection against every kind of data loss.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NoSQL<\/strong> is not a single approach or an automatic\nupgrade. The data structure should suit the actual problem.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For local tools or small online services, <strong>one<\/strong>\ndatabase is often sufficient. Scaling across multiple servers requires\nadditional concepts and introduces new sources of failure.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Next lesson: <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6 –\nNetworks and security<\/a>.<\/strong> There we clarify how multiple\ncomputers exchange data, how browsers find a server and why secure\ntransport connections, authentication and authorisation belong together.\nLater, <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a> explore database topics in\nmore depth, including replication, sharding and distributed\ntransactions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source check:<\/strong> 03.11.2026; contextualisation of\nexternal side effects on 05.11.2026. Technical foundations are largely\nstable over time; product-specific statements have been checked against\nvendor and project documentation. The schema and architecture examples\nare simplified for teaching purposes and are not directly executable\nproduction configurations.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SQLite – <em>Appropriate Uses For SQLite<\/em> (file/embedded\ndatabase and client–server distinction): <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/whentouse.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/whentouse.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SQLite – <em>Query Planning<\/em> (indexes, query plans, search and\nsorting behaviour): <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/queryplanner.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/queryplanner.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SQLite – <em>Foreign Key Support<\/em> (relationships and enabling\nforeign key enforcement): <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SQLite – <em>Transaction<\/em> (transaction control and concurrent\naccess): <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL – <em>Tutorial: The SQL Language<\/em> (SQL, JOIN and\ntables): <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-sql.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-sql.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL – <em>Constraints<\/em> (primary and foreign keys, rules):\n<a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL – <em>Concurrency Control / MVCC<\/em> (concurrent read\nand write operations): <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Cross-references in the course:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3 – Algorithms and data\nstructures<\/a> · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4 – Software\nengineering and system design<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M1-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M1-L6 – Networks and security<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: Foundations of computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: How individual computers become a shared\nsystem<\/h2>\n<p>So far, we have considered programs, computers and databases. As soon\nas two programs on different computers work together, another layer is\nadded: the <strong>network<\/strong>. Data must find their destination,\nbe transmitted correctly along the way and be processed by the\nappropriate application at the destination. Decisions must also be made\nabout who may communicate with whom and which information needs\nprotection.<\/p>\n<p>A browser loading a webpage and a server application querying a\ndatabase represent two sides of the same problem: <strong>reliable\ncommunication<\/strong> and <strong>controlled access<\/strong>. A\ntechnically functioning connection is not enough. A public application\nmust also account for unwanted requests, other users’ accounts, stolen\ncredentials, software errors and data loss.<\/p>\n<p>In this lesson we consider networks as layers, the path of a request\nthrough the internet, the foundations of encryption and security\ndecisions for small applications of your own. The actual operation of a\npublicly accessible server is covered in detail later in M2-L1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The internet is a network of networks<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>local network<\/strong> connects devices in a limited area,\nfor example a desktop computer, a notebook and a router in a flat. The\n<strong>internet<\/strong> consists of very many interconnected networks\noperated by access providers, companies, data centres and other\norganisations. Nobody operates the internet as a single central\nmachine.<\/p>\n<p>Connections between such networks are organised by\n<strong>routers<\/strong>. A router looks at the destination address of a\ndata packet and uses its routing information to decide which next\nstation should receive the packet. This is not the same as a single\nfixed path: routes can change, and data packets can be lost or arrive\nlate.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>PC ── Home network ── Router ── Internet access\n                                    │\n                               Other networks\n                                    │\n                                 Data centre\n                                    │\n                                 Web server<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem does this solve?<\/strong> An application does\nnot need to know which cables, wireless links and routers lie between it\nand its destination. It uses standardised networking functions.\n<strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> If a tool operates entirely\nlocally, neither internet access nor a public address is required.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>packet<\/strong> is a bounded unit of data containing\ncontrol information and payload. Larger transmissions are divided into\nmultiple units at suitable layers; not every packet is the same size. A\nvideo can therefore be carried over the internet even though individual\nnetwork packets are relatively small.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Addresses, names and ports: three different questions<\/h2>\n<p>A network connection must resolve at least three things: <em>Which\ncomputer or network connection? Which application at that destination?\nAnd under which name can a person find the service?<\/em><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"21-ip-adressen-wohin-soll-das-paket\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>IP addresses – where should\nthe packet go?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>IP<\/strong> means <em>Internet Protocol<\/em>. Among other\nthings, it describes the addressing and forwarding of packets across\nmultiple networks. Two address families are commonly encountered:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>IPv4<\/strong> (<em>Internet Protocol Version 4<\/em>): 32-bit\naddresses; a typical notation is <code>192.0.2.10<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>IPv6<\/strong> (<em>Internet Protocol Version 6<\/em>):\n128-bit addresses; an example representation is\n<code>2001:db8::10<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These two addresses are <strong>documentation examples<\/strong>, not\nactual destination servers. The key distinction is between\n<strong>public<\/strong> addresses, which can in principle be routed on\nthe internet, and <strong>private or local<\/strong> address ranges used\nwithin networks. A local address does not automatically mean complete\nprotection; actual access paths depend on the network configuration.<\/p>\n<p>Many home routers use <strong>NAT<\/strong> (<em>Network Address\nTranslation<\/em>). They translate addresses and often ports at the\nboundary between local and public networks. This allows several devices,\nfor example, to share an IPv4 internet connection. NAT is, however,\n<strong>not a security strategy in its own right<\/strong> and is not the\nsame as a firewall.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Notebook: private address ──┐\n                            ├── Home router / NAT ── Public connection\nDesktop: private address ───┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<h3 id=\"22-dns-welcher-name-gehoert-zu-welcher-adresse\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>DNS – which name\ncorresponds to which address?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>DNS<\/strong> stands for <em>Domain Name System<\/em>. It helps\nresolve a human-readable name such as <code>example.org<\/code> into\nsuitable technical address information. A browser does not necessarily\nknow a domain’s destination address in advance. It may first use a local\ncache or query a DNS resolver.<\/p>\n<p>A typical, simplified sequence is:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser: &quot;example.org&quot;\n           │\n           ▼\nLocal DNS cache / DNS resolver\n           │\n           ▼\nIP address of the destination\n           │\n           ▼\nEstablish connection to a server<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An <strong>A record<\/strong> points to an IPv4 address, an\n<strong>AAAA record<\/strong> to an IPv6 address; other DNS record types\nserve other purposes. DNS itself <strong>does not carry the\nwebpage<\/strong>. It is an auxiliary service for name resolution. A\ndomain can also return different addresses depending on the query, for\nexample for regional distribution or load balancing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> For a purely local service,\na direct local address may suffice. For public services, a domain name\nfacilitates access and can hide changed server addresses from users. A\ndomain name does not, however, establish that the service behind it is\ntrustworthy.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"23-ports-welches-programm-soll-die-daten-erhalten\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ports – which\nprogram should receive the data?<\/h3>\n<p>A computer can run a web server, a database service and other\napplications at the same time. <strong>Ports<\/strong> are logical\nnumbers used to associate network connections with a particular service\nor communication endpoint. A port is not a physical socket.<\/p>\n<p>Common assignments are <strong>80<\/strong> for unencrypted HTTP and\n<strong>443<\/strong> for HTTPS. These are conventions, not immutable\nlaws of nature. Not every application on a given port is automatically a\nparticular service; ports must be considered together with the transport\ntechnology used.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Same server address\n   ├── Port 443  → HTTPS web service\n   ├── Port 22   → SSH administration if required (authorised access only)\n   └── Internal database port → preferably not exposed publicly<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>SSH<\/strong> means <em>Secure Shell<\/em>, a protocol for\nsecure remote access. The fact that a service could technically be\naccessible does not mean that it <em>should<\/em> be publicly accessible.\nA small web service usually provides only the public entry point it\nactually needs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The layers of network communication<\/h2>\n<p>Why are there so many abbreviations? Because different problems are\nsolved separately. Physical transmission, packet forwarding, transport\nbetween programs and the actual application protocol are different\ntasks.<\/p>\n<p>The classical <strong>OSI model<\/strong> (<em>Open Systems\nInterconnection<\/em>) distinguishes seven conceptual layers. For our\npurposes, a coarser, practical division is sufficient for now:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer<\/th>\n<th>Guiding question<\/th>\n<th>Examples<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Application<\/td>\n<td>What should be exchanged?<\/td>\n<td>HTTP, DNS, application API<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transport<\/td>\n<td>How do programs communicate?<\/td>\n<td>TCP, UDP, QUIC<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internet/network<\/td>\n<td>How do packets find other networks?<\/td>\n<td>IPv4, IPv6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local transmission<\/td>\n<td>How do data reach the next device?<\/td>\n<td>Ethernet, Wi-Fi, fibre-optic link<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The limits of this simplified representation matter:\n<strong>QUIC<\/strong> is a modern transport protocol based on UDP that\nalso integrates functions for securing connections; <strong>TLS<\/strong>\ndoes not simply belong to one fixed OSI layer in every case. Models aid\nunderstanding; they are not an exact blueprint for every modern\ntechnology.<\/p>\n<p>The principle of layering makes it possible, for example, to access\nthe same web application over a local Wi-Fi network, a mobile connection\nor a fibre-optic connection without reinventing the application for each\nconnection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>TCP, UDP and QUIC: transport with different objectives<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"41-tcp-geordnete-und-zuverlaessige-uebertragung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>TCP – ordered and\nreliable transmission<\/h3>\n<p><strong>TCP<\/strong> means <em>Transmission Control Protocol<\/em>. It\nprovides applications with an <strong>ordered byte stream<\/strong>. In\nnormal operation, TCP ensures that lost segments are retransmitted and\nthat the application receives bytes in the correct order. Connections\ncan of course still fail; TCP does not guarantee that a remote server\nwill never fail.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application A ── Data stream over TCP ── Application B\n    │                                         │\n    └──── Transmission errors are remedied ────┘\n          as far as possible<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>TCP is useful when an application requires an ordered data stream.\nEven with a well-functioning TCP connection, delays can occur, and the\napplication must still handle broken connections.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"42-udp-wenig-vorgaben-keine-eingebaute-zustellgarantie\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>UDP –\nfew requirements, no built-in delivery guarantee<\/h3>\n<p><strong>UDP<\/strong> stands for <em>User Datagram Protocol<\/em>. It\ntransmits separate datagrams, but itself provides neither reliable\ndelivery nor ordering nor retransmission of lost packets. Applications\nand protocols built on it can add such functions as needed.<\/p>\n<p>It is useful when low protocol overhead or custom control is\nparticularly important, for example in certain real-time transmissions.\n<strong>UDP does not automatically mean faster or less secure:<\/strong>\nwhat matters is the complete application and transport design.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"43-quic-und-http3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>QUIC and HTTP/3<\/h3>\n<p><strong>QUIC<\/strong> is a modern transport protocol built on UDP\nthat combines reliable data streams, connection management and\ncryptographic protection. <strong>HTTP/3<\/strong> uses QUIC. The\nstatement “HTTP always runs over TCP” would therefore be incorrect\ntoday. HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 generally use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over\nUDP.<\/p>\n<p>The course objectives do not require you to implement the protocol\nyourself. The important idea is that <strong>the web protocol and the\ntechnology underneath it are distinguishable layers<\/strong>, and new\nprotocols can change the transport technology without requiring all\nwebpages to be redeveloped.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security limitation of accelerated connections:<\/strong> When\nreconnecting, under certain conditions QUIC can transmit data using\n<strong>0-RTT<\/strong> (<em>Zero Round Trip Time<\/em>, early application\ndata before the new handshake is complete). Such early data can under\ncertain conditions be <strong>replayed<\/strong> (<em>replay<\/em>).\nConsequently, without additional safeguards, 0-RTT requests are\nunsuitable for actions with real effects, such as “debit money” or\n“start a paid job”. A protected transport channel does not automatically\nmake an unsafe domain operation resistant to replay. <strong>The feature\nis optional:<\/strong> applications can restrict or disable 0-RTT; it is\nnot a feature of every HTTPS connection. Basis: IETF <a\nhref=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9001.html\">RFC 9001<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>,\nparticularly concerning replay risk. The core TLS 1.3 standard is <a\nhref=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9846.html\">RFC 9846<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>HTTP and HTTPS: the language of the web<\/h2>\n<p><strong>HTTP<\/strong> means <em>Hypertext Transfer Protocol<\/em>. It\ndescribes how a client makes a request and a server responds. A browser\nis a typical client; a program of your own or an AI agent can also send\nHTTP requests.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser                          Web server\n   │                                │\n   ├── Request: GET /artikel ──────►│\n   │                                │\n   ◄──────── Status + content ──────┤\n   │                                │<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><code>GET<\/code> denotes a method for retrieving information.\n<code>POST<\/code> is typically used to submit or process data. Response\ncodes indicate the result, for example <code>200<\/code> (successful),\n<code>404<\/code> (resource not found) or <code>500<\/code> (internal\nserver error). The code alone does not explain every cause.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>API<\/strong> (<em>Application Programming Interface<\/em>)\nspecifies which functions an application offers to other programs. A\n<strong>web API<\/strong> often uses HTTP. An API is not automatically\npublic, however; it can be internal, protected, chargeable or accessible\nonly to particular clients.<\/p>\n<p>In practice, <strong>HTTPS<\/strong> means <em>HTTP over TLS<\/em>,\nthat is, HTTP over a connection protected by <strong>TLS<\/strong>. TLS\nmeans <em>Transport Layer Security<\/em>. HTTPS is intended to protect\ncommunications in transit against eavesdropping and undetected\nmodification and helps verify the identity of the server being\ncontacted.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ── HTTP requests within a protected TLS connection ── Server<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> HTTPS does not automatically make a\nwebsite trustworthy, a database secure or an application free of\nprogramming errors. It primarily protects the connection between the\nrespective TLS endpoints. If TLS terminates at an upstream proxy, for\nexample, further internal transmission paths must be considered\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cryptography: hashes, encryption, keys and certificates<\/h2>\n<p>The term <strong>cryptography<\/strong> describes mathematical methods\nthat can, for example, keep information confidential, check it for\nchanges or associate it with a sender. Different methods serve different\npurposes. Four terms are particularly often confused.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"61-hashfunktionen-eine-art-prueffingerabdruck\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hash functions: a\nkind of verification fingerprint<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>cryptographic hash function<\/strong> transforms data of\narbitrary length into a fixed-length value. If the input data change,\nthe hash value also changes with very high probability. Such methods are\nused, among other things, to check the integrity of a file.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>File &quot;lektion.md&quot; ── Hash function ──► Checksum A\nModified file     ── Hash function ──► Checksum B\n                                             A ≠ B<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Importantly, a hash value is <strong>not encryption<\/strong> that\ncould simply be decrypted afterwards. Nor is it evidence of <em>who<\/em>\ncreated the file. If an attacker can replace the file and published\nchecksum at the same time, checking the hash is insufficient to\nestablish authenticity.<\/p>\n<p>Passwords should not be stored in plain text. Special, deliberately\ncomputationally expensive <strong>password-suitable hashing/key\nderivation methods<\/strong> are used for this purpose, for example\nArgon2id, each with an individual <strong>salt<\/strong> (an additional\nrandom value). A fast standard hash such as SHA-256 alone is unsuitable\nbecause automated trial of password candidates would be too fast. The\nOWASP security guidance discusses these differences in detail.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"62-symmetrische-verschluesselung-derselbe-geheime-schluessel\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Symmetric\nencryption: the same secret key<\/h3>\n<p>In <strong>symmetric encryption<\/strong>, both parties use the same\nsecret key to encrypt and decrypt. This is efficient for large amounts\nof data. In this model, anyone who obtains the key can in principle also\ndecrypt data protected by it.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Plaintext + secret key ──► Ciphertext\nCiphertext + same key  ──► Plaintext<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>One practical problem is that the two communication partners must\nfirst securely obtain a shared secret key. Another risk is storage: if\nthe only key is lost, one’s own encrypted data may become unreadable; if\nit is stolen, protection may be lost.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"63-asymmetrische-kryptografie-oeffentliches-und-privates-schluesselpaar\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Asymmetric\ncryptography: public and private key pair<\/h3>\n<p>In <strong>asymmetric cryptography<\/strong> there is a <strong>public\nkey<\/strong> and a corresponding <strong>private key<\/strong>. Depending\non the method, these can be used to encrypt data, agree on key material\nor create and verify digital signatures. Not every asymmetric method\nsupports all of these functions.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>digital signature<\/strong> helps verify whether data were\nsigned using the corresponding private key and remained unchanged after\nsigning. However, the signature alone does not establish a person’s real\nidentity: for that, the public key must be reliably associated with an\nidentity.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"64-zertifikate-und-tls\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Certificates and TLS<\/h3>\n<p>A digital <strong>certificate<\/strong> associates a public key with\ncertain identity information, for example a domain name, and is\ntypically attested by a trusted <strong>certificate authority<\/strong>\n(CA). With HTTPS, the client checks, among other things, whether the\ncertificate applies to the name being contacted and whether the chain of\ntrust and validity conditions are appropriate.<\/p>\n<p>TLS combines several cryptographic mechanisms: in the\n<strong>handshake<\/strong> (initial negotiation), parameters are agreed,\nthe peer is authenticated and shared session keys are derived. Symmetric\nmethods then protect the transmitted payload efficiently. It is\ntherefore an oversimplification to claim that with HTTPS the entire\ntransmission is permanently encrypted “with the public key”.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1. Browser connects to server\n2. TLS handshake: parameters, key agreement, identity verification\n3. Protected communication channel is established\n4. HTTP messages are transmitted over it<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Note on current standards (check date 03.11.2026):<\/strong>\nThe authoritative published text for TLS version 1.3 is <strong>RFC 9846\n(2026)<\/strong>. It supersedes the earlier specification RFC 8446 (2018)\nwithout changing the basic idea of TLS 1.3 explained here. RFC stands\nfor <em>Request for Comments<\/em>, a document series for technical\ninternet specifications.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Authentication and authorisation – two different\ndecisions<\/h2>\n<p>An application with user accounts must distinguish between two\nquestions:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Authentication:<\/strong> <em>Who do you claim to be, and how\ndo we verify it?<\/em><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Authorisation:<\/strong> <em>What is this identified account\npermitted to do?<\/em><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A user may be correctly signed in and still have no access to a\nparticular record. This second check is frequently overlooked, for\nexample when an application checks only whether there is a login at all,\nbut not who owns the requested document.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Request to /api/dokument/4711\n             │\n             ▼\nAuthentication: valid account?\n             │\n             ▼\nAuthorisation: does document 4711 belong to this account?\n             │\n       ┌─────┴─────┐\n       │           │\n      Yes          No\n       │           │\n     Result    Deny access<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>session<\/strong> associates multiple requests with a\nsigned-in user. A session cookie or access token is often used for this.\nSuch values are security-sensitive: anyone who obtains them improperly\nmay in some circumstances impersonate the user. Secure storage, limited\nvalidity and suitable logout or revocation options are therefore part of\nthe design.<\/p>\n<p><strong>MFA<\/strong> (<em>Multi-Factor Authentication<\/em>) combines\nat least two <em>different<\/em> factors, for example a password and a\nhardware security key. A second password is not automatically a second\nfactor. MFA makes certain kinds of account takeover more difficult, but\ndoes not prevent every attack.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What problem do accounts and permissions solve?<\/strong> They\nallow data and features to be associated with particular people.\n<strong>When are they unnecessary?<\/strong> An entirely local offline\ntool without separate users usually does not need a web-based user\naccount. Accounts create additional responsibilities: password\nprotection, recovery, blocking, data protection and support.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Threat modelling: security begins with the right\nquestion<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>threat model<\/strong> is a structured consideration of\n<em>what needs protection, who might attack it, through which means and\nwith what consequences<\/em>. This is usually more helpful than\nindiscriminately incorporating as many security products as\npossible.<\/p>\n<p>Consider an online analysis tool to which users upload files. What\nwould be critical there?<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset<\/th>\n<th>Possible threat<\/th>\n<th>Appropriate basic measure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>User files<\/td>\n<td>Another user reads them<\/td>\n<td>Per-file authorisation, separate\nstorage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User account<\/td>\n<td>Takeover through stolen credentials<\/td>\n<td>Secure session, MFA where appropriate,\nabuse prevention<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server<\/td>\n<td>Attacker exploits unpatched software<\/td>\n<td>Updates, as few exposed services as\npossible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application<\/td>\n<td>Malicious input causes errors<\/td>\n<td>Input validation, secure libraries,\ntests<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Availability<\/td>\n<td>Excessive requests overload the\nservice<\/td>\n<td>Rate limiting, capacity planning,\nmonitoring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data store<\/td>\n<td>Errors or deletion destroy data<\/td>\n<td>Separate backups and recovery testing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>It does not follow from this model that a single measure provides\ncomplete protection. A firewall, for example, does not automatically\nprevent a flaw in a publicly accessible web application. HTTPS helps\nagainst eavesdropping in transit, not against faulty authorisation\nchecks in the backend.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"81-least-privilege-so-wenig-rechte-wie-noetig\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Least privilege –\nonly as many permissions as necessary<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Least privilege<\/strong> means the principle of granting\nminimal permissions. Every person and every technical service should\npossess only the permissions necessary for their task.<\/p>\n<p>For example, a public web process normally needs neither full\nadministrative access to the operating system nor access to all backups.\nAn analysis worker may need access to a single job, but not to the\ncomplete account details of all users.<\/p>\n<p>Closely related is <strong>deny by default<\/strong>: if a permission\nhas not been explicitly granted, access is denied. This is safer than\ninitially permitting all access and prohibiting only some of it.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Internet\n   │\n   ▼\nPublic HTTPS entry point\n   │\n   ▼\nApplication with limited permissions\n   │\n   ├── Access only to assigned jobs\n   └── Database account without administrative privileges<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>When is it overengineered?<\/strong> Complex role management\nwith multiple tenants is unnecessary for a small offline utility. The\nunderlying principle of avoiding unnecessary permissions remains useful\neven there.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"82-firewall-updates-und-sicherheitsgrenzen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Firewalls, updates\nand security limitations<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>firewall<\/strong> allows or blocks network traffic\naccording to rules. For a public service it is generally sensible to\nexpose only necessary entry points. This does not, however, replace\nprogram checks or a secure configuration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security updates<\/strong> close known vulnerabilities. Not\nonly the operating system but also web servers, database systems,\nprogram libraries and one’s own application may be affected. Updates\nentail work and can cause compatibility problems. They must therefore be\nplanned and, for critical systems, rolled out in a verifiable\nmanner.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Backups<\/strong> protect against data loss, for example\nthrough hardware failure, operator error or certain kinds of malware.\nThey are reliable only if copies are kept separately, protected against\nunauthorised changes and recovery is occasionally tested. A continuously\nsynchronised copy is not automatically a suitable backup: deletions and\ncorrupted data can also be synchronised.<\/p>\n<p>A straightforward principle follows: the more services and users a\nsystem has, the more ongoing maintenance and security responsibility it\ncreates — even if the original program code is small.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A complete visit to a secure website<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_007_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"TLS 1.3 over TCP: key shares in ClientHello and ServerHello, followed by encrypted authentication and HTTP application data.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">TLS 1.3 over TCP: key shares in\nClientHello and ServerHello, followed by encrypted authentication and\nHTTP application data.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustration: Simplified TLS 1.3 handshake with server\ncertificate over TCP. The diagram combines server authentication and\nseveral handshake messages. HTTP/3 uses QUIC.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We combine the concepts into a simplified sequence. It is\ndeliberately not a mandatory sequence for all browser protocols but a\ntypical architectural model.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User enters: https://example.org/werkzeug\n                       │\n                       ▼\n                  DNS resolution\n                       │\n                       ▼\n              Destination IP address\n                       │\n                       ▼\n         Establish connection (e.g. TCP)\n                       │\n                       ▼\n         TLS handshake / certificate check\n                       │\n                       ▼\n                 HTTPS request\n                       │\n                       ▼\n                 Web server / API\n                       │\n                       ▼\n          Application logic / permissions\n                       │\n                       ▼\n                   Database\n                       │\n                       ▼\n            HTTPS response to the user<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>With <strong>HTTP/3<\/strong>, transport would run over QUIC rather\nthan a classical TCP connection; the architectural idea remains\ncomparable. Upstream components such as a reverse proxy, caches or a\ncontent delivery network may also be involved. We discuss these later in\nmodule M2.<\/p>\n<p>At each point a different technology answers a different question:\nDNS finds technical addresses; IP transports packets between networks;\nthe transport layer organises communication; TLS protects the channel;\nHTTP describes requests and responses; the application decides on\npermissions and content; the database manages its data.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why this separation matters:<\/strong> When troubleshooting,\n“the website does not work” can have very different causes — from an\nincorrect DNS record or an expired certificate to a faulty database\nquery. Someone who understands the layers does not look for the cause\nindiscriminately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three reference points for your own software ideas<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"101-lokales-programm-mit-sqlite\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local program with SQLite<\/h3>\n<p>A PureBasic tool that reads local files and uses an SQLite database\nneeds <strong>no public network architecture<\/strong> for its core\nfunction. If no data leave the device, many problems associated with\npublic endpoints disappear. Local security, file access protection and\nbackups remain relevant nonetheless.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"102-kleine-oeffentliche-webanwendung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Small public web\napplication<\/h3>\n<p>A simple PHP or JavaScript backend could already run on a single\nrented server. It typically needs a DNS record, an HTTPS entry point,\napplication logic and possibly a database. User accounts additionally\nrequire secure authentication and permission checks.<\/p>\n<p>The one-off development steps are not enough: ongoing costs and tasks\ninclude hosting, a domain and additional services depending on the\nprovider, security updates, certificate management, monitoring, backups\nand assistance with user problems. Managed services exist for some of\nthese tasks; they reduce work but do not completely remove the\noperator’s responsibility for the application.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"103-ki-verarbeitungsdienst-mit-hintergrundauftraegen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI\nprocessing service with background jobs<\/h3>\n<p>A service that processes uploaded images using an AI model has\nadditional sensitive interfaces: files are uploaded, jobs passed on,\nintermediate results stored and later delivered again. Permissions must\nbe correct <strong>throughout the entire process<\/strong>, not only at\nthe first web form.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer\n  │ HTTPS\n  ▼\nAPI / account and access check\n  │\n  ▼\nJob queue ──────────► AI worker\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                    Result store\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                  Protected delivery<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This also illustrates why a larger system is not automatically\nbetter: for a few occasional jobs, a single computer with a queue can be\nsimpler. Many workers running in parallel make sense only when load,\nresilience or particular technical requirements justify the effort.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Typical misconceptions and brief answers<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Common statement<\/th>\n<th>More precise explanation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“A domain is an IP address.”<\/td>\n<td>A domain is a name whose DNS records can\npoint to addresses, among other things.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“HTTPS protects the entire\napplication.”<\/td>\n<td>HTTPS protects the transport channel;\napplication errors and incorrectly assigned permissions are still\npossible.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Anyone signed in can see\neverything.”<\/td>\n<td>Authentication and access permission must\nbe checked separately.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“A hash is encrypted text.”<\/td>\n<td>Hashing is fundamentally not reversible\nencryption.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“NAT replaces a firewall.”<\/td>\n<td>NAT is address translation, not a complete\nsecurity concept.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“TCP guarantees every operation will\nsucceed.”<\/td>\n<td>TCP protects the data stream during a\nfunctioning connection; applications must handle failures\nthemselves.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Replication is a backup.”<\/td>\n<td>Copies can inherit the same deletions or\nerrors; recoverability must be planned separately.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Every small application needs a cloud\narchitecture.”<\/td>\n<td>A local program or a single server may be\nthe more appropriate solution.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M1-L7<\/h2>\n<p>The path through a network consists of several separate tasks:\n<strong>resolving names, finding addresses, forwarding packets,\ntransporting data, interpreting messages and protecting access<\/strong>.\nThe internet is an interconnection of many networks; IP, DNS, TCP or\nUDP, HTTP and TLS each perform different tasks. Security does not arise\nfrom encryption alone, but also from reliable identity verification,\ncorrectly assigned access permissions, up-to-date software, limited\nattack surfaces and recoverable data.<\/p>\n<p>For a small local computer, much of this is unnecessary. Once other\npeople access an application online, however, these questions become\npart of ongoing operation. <strong>M2-L1<\/strong> later shows how to\nturn a local program into a publicly accessible service;\n<strong>M4-L3<\/strong> explores organisational and data protection\nresponsibilities in greater depth.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>next lesson M1-L7<\/strong> completes the overview of\ncomputer science foundations: concurrency, formal languages, distributed\nsystems, human–computer interaction and an introduction to AI before\nmodules M5 and M6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Research/check date: 03.11.2026; clarification of 0-RTT on\n05.11.2026.<\/strong> The links help trace individual technical\nfoundations and security principles. No current provider prices or\nbinding legal advice are asserted.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>MDN Web Docs – Overview of HTTP<\/strong> (request–response\nprinciple, client–server): <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Overview\">https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Overview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>MDN Web Docs – What is a Domain Name?<\/strong> (DNS, domains\nand address resolution): <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Howto/Web_mechanics/What_is_a_domain_name\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Howto/Web_mechanics/What_is_a_domain_name<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>IETF / RFC Editor – RFC 9846: TLS 1.3<\/strong> (valid\nsuccessor specification to RFC 8446): <a\nhref=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9846/\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9846/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>IETF / RFC Editor – RFC 9001: Using TLS to Secure\nQUIC<\/strong> (0-RTT application data and replay risk): <a\nhref=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9001/\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9001/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>OWASP Cheat Sheet Series – Authorization<\/strong> (least\nprivilege, deny by default): <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>OWASP Cheat Sheet Series – Password Storage<\/strong>\n(password-suitable hashing methods and salt): <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>OWASP Cheat Sheet Series – Multifactor\nAuthentication<\/strong> (independent authentication factors): <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Multifactor_Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Multifactor_Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Cloudflare Learning Center – OSI model<\/strong> (layers as\nconceptual abstraction): <a\nhref=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/de-de/learning/ddos/glossary/open-systems-interconnection-model-osi/\">https://www.cloudflare.com/de-de/learning/ddos/glossary/open-systems-interconnection-model-osi/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Network standards and security guidelines\ncan evolve. This lesson explains enduring concepts; specific\nimplementation decisions should be checked again against current\ndocumentation when operating a real system.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M1-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M1-L7 – Overview of further typical Bachelor’s subject areas<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: Foundations of computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: What else belongs to a computer science\ndegree?<\/h2>\n<p>In M1-L1 to M1-L6, we have already learnt the foundations of\nprogramming, computers, algorithms, software architecture, databases and\nnetworks. A computer science degree also considers several questions\nthat may initially seem quite unrelated: How do we prevent errors when\ntwo tasks manipulate the same data simultaneously? How can a computer\nunderstand a programming language? How do programs running on several\ncomputers work? Why is designing a user interface just as much a part of\ncomputer science as an algorithm? And how does machine learning differ\nfrom ordinary programming?<\/p>\n<p>This lesson connects five such areas. It is not intended to replace\nlater specialist courses, but to provide a <strong>map<\/strong>. The\noverview follows the knowledge areas of the <em>CS2023 Computer Science\nCurricula<\/em> by ACM, IEEE Computer Society and AAAI: Parallel and\nDistributed Computing, Foundations of Programming Languages,\nHuman-Computer Interaction, Artificial Intelligence, and Society,\nEthics, and the Profession are explicitly included in the discipline.\nIndividual universities may organise the content differently.<\/p>\n<p>One recurring idea is more important than all the technical terms:\n<strong>As soon as data, time, several actors or people are involved, it\nis often no longer sufficient to consider only the ‘normal’ program\nflow.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concurrency: When several things happen at once<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"definition-and-initial-problem\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Definition and initial\nproblem<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Concurrency<\/strong> means that several tasks can make\nprogress during overlapping periods. <strong>Parallelism<\/strong> refers\nto actual simultaneous execution on several computational units. A\nsingle processor core can alternate between concurrent tasks; multiple\ncores can execute them simultaneously. Concurrency therefore does not\nautomatically mean faster computation.<\/p>\n<p>Why is it needed? A desktop program, for example, should continue to\nrespond to mouse movements while loading a file. A web server must be\nable to serve multiple incoming requests. An AI service may want to\nprepare the next job while a graphics card is already computing. If each\nactivity blocked all others, the application would appear unusable or\nits resources would be poorly utilised.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"processes-threads-and-asynchronous-execution\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Processes, threads\nand asynchronous execution<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>process<\/strong> is a running instance of a program with a\ngenerally separate virtual address space. A <strong>thread<\/strong> is a\nflow of execution within a process; threads belonging to the same\nprocess can use shared memory areas. With an\n<strong>asynchronous<\/strong> approach, a program does not necessarily\nhave to wait idly for a lengthy operation: it can carry out other work\nand process the result later. Asynchronicity does not automatically mean\nthat a separate thread is started or that CPU computation takes place in\nparallel.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application\n   │\n   ├─ Accept user interaction ───────────────┐\n   ├─ Load file ───────── [Waiting time] ────┤\n   └─ Prepare data ──────────────────────────┤\n                                            ▼\n                                      Display result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The sketch shows logical activities; their actual allocation to\nthreads or processor cores depends on the runtime environment used.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"the-race-condition-two-correct-operations-produce-an-incorrect-result\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The\nrace condition: Two correct operations produce an incorrect result<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>race condition<\/strong> occurs when the result depends on\nthe accidental order in which accesses take place. Suppose a counter\nstands at 10. Two tasks are each meant to add 1:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Initial value: 10\n\nTask A: reads 10 ─────────────────┐\nTask B:          reads 10 ────────┤\nTask A: writes 11 ────────────────┤\nTask B:                  writes 11┘\n\nExpected: 12       Actually possible: 11<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Viewed separately, both tasks appear to have calculated correctly.\nThe problem is the shared state. The example is also relevant because a\nsingle programming expression such as ‘increment the counter by one’ is\nnot guaranteed to be an indivisible operation in every context.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"synchronisation-locks-and-atomic-steps\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synchronisation, locks\nand atomic steps<\/h3>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_008_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Comparison of two threads with and without a lock: lost update in parallel account transactions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of two threads with and\nwithout a lock: lost update in parallel account\ntransactions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustration: Example of a lost update: both threads read 100 and\nwrite 150 without synchronisation. A correctly shared lock serialises\nthe changes to 200; here it protects only the processes concerned within\nthe respective shared locking scope.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Synchronisation<\/strong> coordinates access by multiple tasks\nto shared data. A <strong>mutex<\/strong> (<em>mutual exclusion<\/em>)\nallows only one flow of execution at a time to enter a protected\nsection. An <strong>atomic operation<\/strong> takes effect as an\nindivisible step with respect to competing accesses. Database\ntransactions can address similar protection requirements at database\nlevel, but are not a blanket solution to all concurrency in a\nprogram.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Task A ──► Acquire lock ──► Change counter ──► Release lock\n                                ▲\nTask B ───────── waits ─────────┘ ──────────► Change counter<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Locks can prevent errors, but also cost time. Excessively large\nlocking scopes reduce parallelism; poorly chosen locking rules can\ncreate new errors. Sometimes it is even simpler to <strong>avoid shared\nmutable state<\/strong>: each task works on its own copy of the data and\nhands over a result only at the end.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Distinction from a distributed system:<\/strong> A lock in the\nmemory of a process initially protects only the participating flows of\nexecution in that specific coordination scope. Two separate servers with\ntheir own locks can nevertheless start the same external action. The\nshared data store or the external service being called must then ensure\nthe uniqueness of the business effect; a local mutex alone is\ninsufficient. The later lesson <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a> explores failure models for\nqueues and workers in more detail.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"deadlock-starvation-and-livelock\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Deadlock, starvation and\nlivelock<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>deadlock<\/strong> occurs when several tasks wait for one\nanother and can therefore no longer make progress. Example: task A holds\nresource 1 and waits for resource 2; task B holds resource 2 and waits\nfor resource 1.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Task A ── holds file X ── waits for database Y\n   ▲                                  │\n   │                                  ▼\nTask B ◄─ waits for file X ◄─ holds database Y<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The labels illustrate the classic circular wait; the file and\ndatabase are merely resources chosen to make it concrete.\nCountermeasures include a consistent order for requesting locks, short\ncritical sections and targeted timeouts. <strong>Starvation<\/strong>\nmeans that a task has no fair opportunity to obtain a required resource\nfor a prolonged period. In a <strong>livelock<\/strong>, tasks still\nactively respond to one another but make no useful progress.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When useful?<\/strong> Concurrency helps with large numbers of\nusers, background processing and responsive interaction. <strong>When\nunnecessary?<\/strong> For a small utility processing a few files in\nsequence, additional threads often only create more opportunities for\nerrors. More threads are not a free performance improvement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Formal languages, automata and compilers<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"why-computers-need-rules-for-language\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why computers need rules\nfor language<\/h3>\n<p>People often recognise from context what is meant. A computer needs\nprecise rules for program code and structured data: Which character\nsequences are permitted? What does an expression mean? Which sequence of\ninstructions is possible?<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>formal language<\/strong> is a set of strings that may be\nformed according to specified rules. A <strong>grammar<\/strong>\ndescribes such rules. For example, it can specify that an expression\nconsists of a number, a plus sign and another number. Initially this\ndescribes only the <strong>syntax<\/strong> (form), not the entire\n<strong>semantics<\/strong> (meaning).<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>automaton<\/strong> is a mathematical model that processes\nsymbols or events and changes state in the process. A <strong>finite\nautomaton<\/strong> has only finitely many possible states. It is\nsuitable, for example, for recognising simple text patterns or modelling\na login procedure. A compiler typically uses several successive\nprocessing steps; simple character patterns and nested language\nstructures require different methods.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-user-login-as-a-state-machine\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: User login as a\nstate machine<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>[Logged out] ── valid login ──► [Logged in]\n      ▲                              │\n      └────────── log out ────────────┘\n\n[Logged out] ── invalid details ──► [Logged out]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This simple model forces us to ask which transitions are permitted. A\nreal login system obviously needs further rules, for example for expired\nsessions, multi-factor authentication, locked accounts and error cases.\nA state machine replaces neither password verification nor security\narchitecture; it makes particular processes easier to verify.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"from-source-code-to-a-program\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From source code to a\nprogram<\/h3>\n<p>A typical compiler can conceptually be divided into the following\nsteps:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Source code\n   │\n   ▼\nLexical analysis (characters → tokens)\n   │\n   ▼\nSyntax analysis / parsing (tokens → structure)\n   │\n   ▼\nSemantic checks (meaning, types, names)\n   │\n   ▼\nIntermediate representation and optimisation (optional)\n   │\n   ▼\nCode generation (e.g. machine code)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>token<\/strong> here is a recognised linguistic unit, such\nas a numeric literal, an identifier or an operator.\n<strong>Parsing<\/strong> is the checking and structuring of input\naccording to grammar rules. A semantic check might establish, for\nexample, that an undefined identifier is being used. Not every\nprogramming language and runtime environment uses exactly the same\npipeline: interpretation, bytecode and just-in-time compilation may take\ndifferent routes.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"regular-expressions-are-not-universal-parsers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Regular\nexpressions are not universal parsers<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Regular expressions<\/strong> (often abbreviated to\n<strong>regex<\/strong>) are especially suitable for recognising and\nbreaking down many clearly defined character patterns. They are not a\nsuitable replacement for a parser for all arbitrarily nested syntactic\nstructures. Common regex implementations also provide extensions that go\nbeyond classical regular languages; the precise expressive scope depends\non the system used.<\/p>\n<p>For a simple filename validator or log search, a few patterns are\noften sufficient. For a complete programming language, a complex\ndocument grammar or strictly nested data formats, proper syntax analysis\nis more appropriate.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When useful?<\/strong> Parsers, data formats, programming\nlanguages and verifiable business processes benefit from formal rules.\n<strong>When excessive?<\/strong> Simple, clearly documented conditions\noften suffice for a very small tool. Developing a compiler specifically\nfor that would not be an improvement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distributed systems: A program no longer runs in just one\nplace<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"begriff-und-ausgangsproblem-2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Definition and initial\nproblem<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>distributed system<\/strong> consists of several independent\ncomputers or processes that interact via messages to offer a common\nservice. They normally do not share directly accessible main memory.\nEach participant sees only its own state and messages it has\nreceived.<\/p>\n<p>Why distribute software? Possible reasons include geographical\ndistance, greater fault tolerance, different specialised hardware or\nmore computing capacity. The difficulty is that each additional computer\nintroduces network latency, transmission errors, independent failures\nand contradictory observations.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User ──► Web server ──► Job queue\n                           │\n                           ├─► Worker A ──► Result store\n                           └─► Worker B ──► Result store\n                                   │\n                              Status database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Workers<\/strong> are background processes that execute tasks.\nA <strong>queue<\/strong> collects jobs that are not processed\nimmediately. This pattern handles lengthy work, such as image\ncomputation or document analysis, while the web server can respond\npromptly to user requests.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"a-message-does-not-yet-guarantee-an-effect\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A message does not\nyet guarantee an effect<\/h3>\n<p>Suppose worker A receives a job to process an image and stores the\nresult, but the acknowledgement to the job service is lost. From the\noutside, it initially looks as if the job failed. A retry could start\nthe same processing a second time.<\/p>\n<p>Distributed applications must therefore distinguish between\n<strong>message delivered<\/strong>, <strong>job started<\/strong>,\n<strong>result stored<\/strong> and <strong>effect completed<\/strong>. A\ncommon design technique is <strong>idempotence<\/strong>: repeated\ndelivery of the same uniquely identified job must not cause an unwanted\nadditional effect. This does not make the retry mechanism itself\nfault-free; the business rules make it more manageable.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"why-time-and-ordering-become-difficult\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why time and ordering\nbecome difficult<\/h3>\n<p>On one computer, a central clock can be helpful. In a network,\ncomputers have their own clocks; messages can take different amounts of\ntime to travel. Two events on different computers cannot therefore\nalways be reliably ordered as ‘first’ and ‘afterwards’ based simply on\nclock time. Computer science uses, among other things, <strong>logical\nclocks<\/strong> and explicit ordering relations for this purpose. Leslie\nLamport’s classic 1978 paper describes such an approach.<\/p>\n<p>For an order, for example, the ordering is crucial: a payment must\nnot be regarded as recorded if the necessary operation was never\ncompleted correctly. For a public image gallery, by contrast, a newly\nadded image becoming visible on a second server only a little later may\noften be acceptable.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"consistency-availability-and-failure\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Consistency, availability\nand failure<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Consistency<\/strong> describes, depending on the model used,\nwhich observations are permitted for multiple participants.\n<strong>Strong consistency<\/strong> may require that certain\nacknowledged modifications become visible to subsequent accesses in a\nwell-defined order. In simplified terms, <strong>eventual\nconsistency<\/strong> means that, in the absence of new changes and under\nthe assumptions of the particular system, copies can eventually agree\nagain. Eventual consistency is not a blanket promise of a fixed\ndelay.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Availability<\/strong> means that a service can respond to\nrequests under certain assumptions. An additional server does not\nautomatically improve availability: shared databases, networks,\nconfigurations or faulty updates can still affect all instances.\nMoreover, in the event of a network failure, it is necessary to\ndistinguish whether another computer has actually failed or is merely\ntemporarily unreachable.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>consensus algorithm<\/strong> helps several nodes agree on a\ncommon decision or ordering despite certain failures. Such algorithms\nrequire clear assumptions about the types of failure and available\nparticipants. We consider the precise interpretation of consistency, the\n<strong>CAP theorem<\/strong> and its limited claim, as well as\nreplication and sharding, in detail only in the database-focused section\nC.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When useful?<\/strong> When a single computer demonstrably no\nlonger suffices or a requirement of the application demands several\nlocations. <strong>When unnecessary?<\/strong> For a small service with\nfew users, a single server with reliable backups, monitoring and clear\nrecovery rules can be more robust and less expensive overall than a\npoorly understood cluster.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Human-computer interaction: Good software is also usable\nsoftware<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"what-does-this-field-investigate\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does this field\ninvestigate?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Human-computer interaction (HCI)<\/strong> investigates how\npeople perceive and operate digital systems and how the design of those\nsystems can be assessed and improved. It is not just about the visual\ndesign of a window. Comprehensibility, error prevention, speed of use,\naccessibility and the expectations of the particular target group also\nmatter.<\/p>\n<p>Software may perform calculations perfectly and still fail in\neveryday use if users cannot find a function, recognise important\ndifferences or notice an incorrect input.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"three-simple-design-rules\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three simple design rules<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Feedback:<\/strong> After an action, it must be clear what has\nhappened. An upload needs a comprehensible status rather than several\nminutes of waiting without any indication.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Consistency:<\/strong> Identical terms and controls should\nwork in the same way. Someone seeing ‘Save’ on one page and ‘Apply’ on\nanother should not have to guess whether the two mean the same\nthing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Error tolerance:<\/strong> A system should prevent errors\nwhere possible and enable recovery where appropriate. An accidentally\ndeleted draft could, for example, initially be moved to the recycle bin,\nprovided that data protection and retention obligations do not preclude\nit.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User intention\n    │\n    ▼\nInterface with comprehensible action\n    │\n    ▼\nApplication validates input\n    │\n    ├─ plausible ──► Action ──► Visible confirmation\n    └─ incorrect ──► Clear message + opportunity to correct<\/code><\/pre>\n<h3\nid=\"accessibility-concerns-more-than-a-small-group-of-users\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Accessibility\nconcerns more than a small group of users<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Accessibility<\/strong> means designing digital content and\nfunctions so that they are, as far as possible, accessible to people\nwith different physical, sensory or cognitive abilities. For web\nofferings, the <strong>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n(WCAG)<\/strong> of the <strong>World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)<\/strong>\nprovide important technical guidance. The four fundamental principles\nare: perceivable, operable, understandable and robust.<\/p>\n<p>Examples include keyboard operability, sufficient contrast, clear\nheadings, alternative text for meaningful images, comprehensible error\nmessages and an interface that still works when enlarged. Not every\nvisual detail must be designed identically; what matters is whether the\nfunction remains accessible.<\/p>\n<p>Such measures often help other people too: someone with only one hand\nfree while travelling benefits from easily accessible controls; someone\nwith little experience of an application benefits from clear\nlanguage.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"when-does-a-user-interface-become-a-cost-factor\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When does a\nuser interface become a cost factor?<\/h3>\n<p>A custom-developed interface requires time for design, error\nhandling, testing on several devices, accessibility and subsequent\nchanges. For a personal utility, a simple table view may be perfectly\nsufficient. For a public offering with many unfamiliar users,\ndocumentation, understandable error texts and ease of support are part\nof product quality and ongoing operating costs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data protection, security and computer science ethics<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"protection-by-design-rather-than-as-an-afterthought\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Protection\nby design rather than as an afterthought<\/h3>\n<p>In M1-L6 we discussed technical security. <strong>Data\nprotection<\/strong> poses a different question: <em>May the data be\nprocessed for this purpose at all, and how much of it is necessary?<\/em>\nAn encrypted database can technically protect personal data and\nnevertheless contain more information than the application needs.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)<\/strong>\nincludes principles such as purpose limitation, data minimisation,\nstorage limitation, transparency, and integrity/confidentiality for\nprocessing to which it applies. The <strong>European Data Protection\nBoard (EDPB)<\/strong> also explains <strong>privacy by design<\/strong>\nand <strong>privacy by default<\/strong>. The obligations that apply in\nindividual cases depend on the specific service, the parties and the\ndata. This lesson is not legal advice.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Plan new function\n       │\n       ▼\nWhich data are genuinely necessary?\n       │\n       ├─ unnecessary ─► Do not collect them in the first place\n       │\n       └─ necessary ───► Define purpose + access rights + deletion rule\n                                │\n                                ▼\n                         Implement technically<\/code><\/pre>\n<h3 id=\"ethics-is-not-simply-the-same-as-compliance-with-the-law\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ethics\nis not simply the same as compliance with the law<\/h3>\n<p>Computer science ethics also deals with questions such as: Does a\nsystem create avoidable disadvantages? Can users understand its\ndecisions? Are people treated unfairly by automated assessments? Who\nbears responsibility if an AI system makes a mistake?<\/p>\n<p>An automated decision can be programmed correctly in formal terms and\nstill be based on biased data. For a model that evaluates people,\nmeasuring average accuracy alone is therefore insufficient. Possible\nconsequences of errors, their distribution, opportunities to contest\ndecisions and the appropriateness of automation must also be\nconsidered.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When essential?<\/strong> Particularly with health data,\nemployment decisions, personal profiles and applications that have\ntangible consequences for third parties in general. <strong>When\nsimpler?<\/strong> A purely local, personal filename converter without\nother people’s personal data requires considerably less organisational\neffort, but not zero technical care.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data processing and machine learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"classical-data-analysis-making-patterns-visible\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Classical data\nanalysis: Making patterns visible<\/h3>\n<p>Data science combines computer science with statistics and domain\nknowledge. A simple analysis program can calculate averages, frequencies\nor trends over time from sales figures. These values are initially\n<strong>descriptive<\/strong>: they say what was observed in the recorded\ndata. They do not establish whether a relationship represents a\ncause.<\/p>\n<p>Important problems arise before computation even begins: missing\ndata, inconsistent measurement methods, duplicate entries, changed\ncategories or biased selection can render results unusable. More data do\nnot automatically solve these problems.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"what-is-machine-learning\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is machine learning?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Machine learning (ML)<\/strong> refers to methods in which a\nmodel adjusts parameters based on example data or feedback, rather than\nspecifying every decision entirely as a fixed rule. With\n<strong>supervised learning<\/strong>, examples have target values, such\nas images and their classes. With <strong>unsupervised\nlearning<\/strong>, structures are investigated in data without such\ntarget assignments. <strong>Reinforcement learning<\/strong> works with\nfeedback on actions and consequences in an environment.<\/p>\n<p>The learning method must suit the problem. A clearly defined tax rate\nor an exactly computable checksum does not need a trained model. For\nvaried images, spoken language or patterns that are difficult to\nformalise, machine learning may be helpful.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Training data ──► Learning algorithm ──► Model parameters\n                                              │\n                                    later: inference\n                                              │\nNew input ────────────────────────────────────┤\n                                              ▼\n                                        Prediction/output<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Training<\/strong> is the model adjustment phase.\n<strong>Inference<\/strong> is the application of a trained model to new\ninputs. A <strong>large language model (LLM)<\/strong> can generate text\nduring inference based on learnt patterns and the current context. This\ndoes not mean that every response represents verified knowledge or a\ngenuine database query. Details follow in M6-L1 to M6-L4.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"what-are-neural-networks-and-deep-learning\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What are neural\nnetworks and deep learning?<\/h3>\n<p>An <strong>artificial neural network<\/strong> is a mathematical model\nwith adjustable weights that processes inputs through several\ncomputational steps. <strong>Deep learning<\/strong> uses multilayer\nnetworks. Many present-day systems for language, image and audio\nprocessing belong to this family. The name is historically inspired by\nthe brain; that does not imply equivalence to biological neurons.<\/p>\n<p>When evaluating a model, it is necessary to distinguish performance\non training data from performance on <strong>new, independent\nexamples<\/strong>. A model may memorise training examples and still\nperform poorly on new data. This is known, among other things, as\n<strong>overfitting<\/strong>. A fair evaluation requires suitable\nassessment data separate from the training phase.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"limits-and-operating-costs\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and operating costs<\/h3>\n<p>A model can make mistakes, respond less effectively to changed data\nor inherit bias from the training data. Large models also involve\ncomputing costs, electricity consumption, memory requirements, model\nversions, possible data leaks and legal questions about training data\nand data supplied by users. For a simple statistic over a few rows of a\ntable, a large neural network would be both more expensive and harder to\nverify than an ordinary algorithm.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three connected application scenarios<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-a-local-media-archive\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario A: Local media\narchive<\/h3>\n<p>A local media catalogue might need a filename parser, a database for\nmetadata and a clear search interface. Concurrency may be useful if a\nlengthy file analysis runs in the background. A server cluster, a neural\nnetwork or several synchronised databases would be excessive without a\nspecific need.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Files ─► Parser ─► Local database ─► Search interface\n               └─ Background analysis (optional)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Operational implication:<\/strong> Manageable effort, but\nbackups and careful handling of faulty files remain necessary.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-b-paid-online-tool\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario B: Paid online tool<\/h3>\n<p>Usability, input validation, access protection and traceable states\nbecome important here. Several concurrent users can modify the same data\nstore. If money, quotas or orders are involved, the rules for competing\nmodifications must be particularly clear. A single host may still be\nsufficient.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User ─► Web interface ─► Application ─► Database\n             │                  │\n       Error feedback     Authorisation check<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Operational implication:<\/strong> Support, security updates,\nbackups, data protection objectives and available resources must be\nplanned. Not every problem is solved by more servers.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-c-ai-job-service-with-several-workers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario C: AI\njob service with several workers<\/h3>\n<p>A queue separates the rapid receipt of a job from its lengthy\nprocessing. Several workers can process different jobs. An identifiable\njob status and idempotent result delivery help manage retries after\nfailures. If the service later controls devices, safety-critical\nreactions must be designed locally and independently of a permanent\ncloud connection.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer ─► API ─► Job queue ─► AI worker\n                       │             │\n                       ▼             ▼\n                 Status management ◄── Result store<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Operational implication:<\/strong> Possible duplicate jobs,\nstorage requirements, data protection for uploaded content, GPU costs\nand failure handling. A distributed design is useful only if its\nbenefits justify the additional complexity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Which field solves which problem?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field<\/th>\n<th>Typical problem<\/th>\n<th>Often unnecessary when …<\/th>\n<th>Later study<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Concurrency<\/td>\n<td>A program must coordinate several\nactivities<\/td>\n<td>There is only a short linear workflow<\/td>\n<td>M2-L2, M3-L5, M4-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formal languages and automata<\/td>\n<td>Inputs and processes must be handled\naccording to clear rules<\/td>\n<td>Simple fixed checks suffice<\/td>\n<td>M2-L2, M6-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distributed systems<\/td>\n<td>Several computers must work together\ncorrectly<\/td>\n<td>One computer meets the requirements<\/td>\n<td>M2-L5, M3-L1–M3-L6, M4-L1–M4-L2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Human-computer interaction<\/td>\n<td>Users cannot reliably understand and\noperate functions<\/td>\n<td>The application is only a small internal\ntool; basic comprehensibility remains necessary<\/td>\n<td>M2-L2, M4-L5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data protection and ethics<\/td>\n<td>Data processing or automation may\nadversely affect people<\/td>\n<td>There are genuinely no relevant\nthird-party data or consequences; careful assessment remains\nnecessary<\/td>\n<td>M4-L3, M6-L3–M6-L7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Machine learning<\/td>\n<td>Patterns are difficult to express\ncompletely as fixed rules<\/td>\n<td>A simple deterministic calculation\nsuffices<\/td>\n<td>M5-L1–M6-L6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary: Completing the foundations block<\/h2>\n<p>Computer science investigates not only individual programs, but also\n<strong>correct collaboration, precise descriptions, distributed state,\ngood usability and learning methods<\/strong>. Concurrency creates\nresponsiveness but also race conditions and deadlocks. Formal languages\nand automata make inputs and states verifiable. Distributed systems\nenable larger or geographically separated services, but introduce\nmessage failures and consistency issues. Human-computer interaction and\ndata protection help determine whether a technical solution is practical\nand responsible. Machine learning extends the possibilities when\nclassical rules are insufficient; it by no means replaces these rules\neverywhere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Research/review date: 03.11.2026.<\/strong> The foundations of\nformal languages, synchronisation and collaboration between computers\nare long-established concepts. Links provide technical orientation and\ndo not substantiate statements about current provider prices. For data\nprotection and specific legal consequences, additional case-by-case\nassessment is necessary.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>ACM / IEEE Computer Society / AAAI – CS2023 Knowledge\nAreas:<\/strong> Overview of typical computer science knowledge areas\n(Parallel/Distributed Computing, HCI, AI, Programming Languages,\nEthics). <a\nhref=\"https://csed.acm.org/knowledge-areas/\">https://csed.acm.org/knowledge-areas/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Oracle Java Tutorials – Synchronization:<\/strong> Shared\nstate, thread interference and synchronisation. The tutorials explicitly\nrefer to Java 8 and serve here only as a conceptual explanation. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/concurrency/sync.html\">https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/concurrency/sync.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Oracle Java Tutorials – Deadlock:<\/strong> Teaching example\nof mutual waiting, also an older Java 8 teaching text. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/concurrency/deadlock.html\">https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/concurrency/deadlock.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Leslie Lamport (1978) – Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of\nEvents in a Distributed System:<\/strong> Foundations of logical event\nordering. <a\nhref=\"https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/time-clocks.pdf\">https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/time-clocks.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>W3C Web Accessibility Initiative – WCAG 2 at a\nGlance:<\/strong> The principles of perceivability, operability,\nunderstandability and robustness. <a\nhref=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/glance/\">https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/glance/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>European Data Protection Board – Principles of\nprocessing:<\/strong> Transparency, purpose limitation, data minimisation\nand other fundamental GDPR principles. <a\nhref=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/topics/key-gdpr-concepts/basic-principles_de\">https://www.edpb.europa.eu/topics/key-gdpr-concepts/basic-principles_de<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>European Data Protection Board – Data protection by design\nand by default:<\/strong> Principle and ongoing consideration. <a\nhref=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/topics/ai-and-technology/privacy-by-design-and-by-default_de\">https://www.edpb.europa.eu/topics/ai-and-technology/privacy-by-design-and-by-default_de<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google for Developers – Machine Learning Crash\nCourse:<\/strong> Introduction to learning methods, data, model training\nand modern AI; the website offers optional exercises that are not\nrequired for this reading course. <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Outlook:<\/strong> The precise guarantees of distributed\ndatabase systems follow in module M3; the technical development of\nneural networks and language models and the path towards robotics follow\nin modules M5 and M6. This lesson provides orientation, not a\nreplacement for these more advanced studies.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
